window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "numerical", "lessons": {"floating-point": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-a-computer-can-t-store-every-number\" data-h=\"3\">Why a computer can't store every number</h3>\n<p>Between 0 and 1 there are infinitely many real numbers, but a computer has a finite amount of memory. A 64-bit number can take at most <span class=\"m\">2^{64}</span> different bit patterns, which is a huge number (about <span class=\"m\">1.8 \\times 10^{19}</span>) but still finite. So the computer must choose a finite set of real numbers it can represent exactly, and round everything else to the nearest one of them. The scheme almost every computer uses is called <strong>floating point</strong>, standardised as IEEE 754. Every neural network, physics simulation and spreadsheet runs on it, and understanding it explains strange results like <code>0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3</code> being false.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a calculator display that shows only four significant digits plus a power of ten, like <span class=\"m\">1.234 \\times 10^{5}</span>. It can show <span class=\"m\">123{,}400</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.0001234</span> equally well, because the decimal point \"floats\" to wherever the digits are. But it cannot show <span class=\"m\">123{,}456</span>: it must round to <span class=\"m\">1.235 \\times 10^{5}</span>, an error of <span class=\"m\">44</span>. Near <span class=\"m\">1</span>, the same display can only step from <span class=\"m\">1.000</span> to <span class=\"m\">1.001</span>, a gap of <span class=\"m\">0.001</span>. Near <span class=\"m\">100{,}000</span> the gap is <span class=\"m\">100</span>. The gaps are large for large numbers and small for small ones, but always about the same <em>relative</em> size, roughly one part in a thousand. Floating point works exactly like this display, except in base 2 and with many more digits.</p>\n<h3 id=\"scientific-notation-in-binary\" data-h=\"3\">Scientific notation in binary</h3>\n<p>A floating-point number is stored as\n<span class=\"mb\">x = (-1)^s \\times 1.f \\times 2^{e},</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">s</span> is a <strong>sign bit</strong>, <span class=\"m\">1.f</span> is the <strong>significand</strong> (also called the mantissa), a binary number between 1 and 2, and <span class=\"m\">e</span> is an integer <strong>exponent</strong>. Because every nonzero binary number can be shifted so it starts with \"1.\", that leading 1 doesn't need to be stored. Only the fraction bits <span class=\"m\">f</span> are kept.</p>\n<p>Let's encode <span class=\"m\">6.5</span>. In binary, <span class=\"m\">6 = 110_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.5 = 0.1_2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">6.5 = 110.1_2</span>. Shift the point two places left: <span class=\"m\">110.1_2 = 1.101_2 \\times 2^2</span>. So the sign is 0, the exponent is 2 and the stored fraction bits are <span class=\"m\">101</span> followed by zeros. Check: <span class=\"m\">1.101_2 = 1 + \\frac12 + \\frac18 = 1.625</span>, and <span class=\"m\">1.625 \\times 4 = 6.5</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"float\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A toy floating-point system: the tick marks are the only representable numbers, and the gaps double at each power of 2.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-standard-formats\" data-h=\"3\">The standard formats</h3>\n<p>IEEE 754 <strong>double precision</strong> (64 bits, <code>float64</code>) uses 1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits and 52 fraction bits. <strong>Single precision</strong> (32 bits, <code>float32</code>) uses 1, 8 and 23. The exponent is stored with a <strong>bias</strong> so that it is never negative: 1023 for double and 127 for single. For <span class=\"m\">6.5</span> in single precision the stored exponent is <span class=\"m\">2 + 127 = 129</span>.</p>\n<p>The number of fraction bits sets the precision. The gap between 1 and the next representable number is called <strong>machine epsilon</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon = 2^{-52} \\approx 2.2 \\times 10^{-16}</span> in double and <span class=\"m\">2^{-23} \\approx 1.2 \\times 10^{-7}</span> in single. That gives about 16 significant decimal digits in double and about 7 in single. The exponent bits set the range: doubles reach about <span class=\"m\">1.8 \\times 10^{308}</span>, singles about <span class=\"m\">3.4 \\times 10^{38}</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Floating-point number</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A number stored as sign, significand and exponent, <span class=\"m\">(-1)^s \\times 1.f \\times 2^e</span>, with a fixed number of bits for each. IEEE 754 double precision uses 64 bits; single precision uses 32.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Machine epsilon</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The gap between 1 and the next larger floating-point number: <span class=\"m\">2^{-52}</span> in double precision, <span class=\"m\">2^{-23}</span> in single. Rounding to nearest has relative error at most half of this, the unit roundoff.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"gaps-grow-with-size\" data-h=\"3\">Gaps grow with size</h3>\n<p>Between consecutive powers of two there are always <span class=\"m\">2^{52}</span> evenly spaced doubles. Between 1 and 2 the spacing is <span class=\"m\">2^{-52}</span>; between 2 and 4 it is <span class=\"m\">2^{-51}</span>; between <span class=\"m\">2^{53} \\approx 9.0 \\times 10^{15}</span> and <span class=\"m\">2^{54}</span> it is exactly 2. So in double precision <span class=\"m\">10^{16} + 1</span> rounds back to <span class=\"m\">10^{16}</span>: the 1 is smaller than half the gap and simply disappears. In general the spacing near <span class=\"m\">x</span> is about <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon|x|</span>, and rounding to the nearest float has <strong>relative error</strong> at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}</span>. This is the single most useful fact about floating point: errors are relative, not absolute.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-0-1-0-2-is-not-0-3\" data-h=\"3\">Why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3</h3>\n<p>In base 10, <span class=\"m\">\\frac13 = 0.333\\ldots</span> never ends. In base 2 the same happens to <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{10}</span>: <span class=\"m\">0.1 = 0.0001100110011\\ldots_2</span>, repeating forever. The computer stores the nearest double, which is slightly more than <span class=\"m\">0.1</span>. The same happens to <span class=\"m\">0.2</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.3</span>, and the rounding errors don't cancel: in double precision <span class=\"m\">0.1 + 0.2</span> gives <span class=\"m\">0.30000000000000004</span>, while the literal <span class=\"m\">0.3</span> is stored as <span class=\"m\">0.29999999999999998\\ldots</span>. The two differ in the last bit. Fractions whose denominator is a power of two, like <span class=\"m\">0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.25</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.375</span>, are stored exactly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"special-values-and-the-edges-of-the-range\" data-h=\"3\">Special values and the edges of the range</h3>\n<p>The standard reserves bit patterns for special cases. A result too large to represent <strong>overflows</strong> to <code>inf</code>; for example <span class=\"m\">e^{710}</span> in double precision is <code>inf</code>, because <span class=\"m\">\\ln(1.8 \\times 10^{308}) \\approx 709.8</span>. A result too small <strong>underflows</strong> gradually through <strong>subnormal</strong> numbers (tiny values with lost precision, down to about <span class=\"m\">4.9 \\times 10^{-324}</span>) and then to 0. Undefined operations like <span class=\"m\">0/0</span> or <span class=\"m\">\\infty - \\infty</span> give <code>NaN</code>, \"not a number\", which spreads through every calculation it touches and is not even equal to itself. There are also two zeros, <span class=\"m\">+0</span> and <span class=\"m\">-0</span>, which compare equal.</p>\n<h3 id=\"arithmetic-is-not-quite-algebra\" data-h=\"3\">Arithmetic is not quite algebra</h3>\n<p>Each operation is computed exactly and then rounded. That small rule breaks familiar laws. Addition is not associative: <span class=\"m\">(10^{16} + 1) - 10^{16} = 0</span>, but <span class=\"m\">10^{16} - 10^{16} + 1 = 1</span>. Adding a long list of small numbers to a big running total can lose them all. Order matters, which is why parallel GPU sums can give slightly different answers from run to run.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Testing floats with <code>==</code>. Compare with a tolerance instead: <span class=\"m\">|a - b| \\le \\text{tol}\\cdot\\max(|a|, |b|)</span>.</li>\n<li>Thinking more decimal places printed means more accuracy. A double has about 16 meaningful digits no matter how many you print.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that integers above <span class=\"m\">2^{53}</span> are not all representable as doubles (and above <span class=\"m\">2^{24}</span> as singles).</li>\n<li>Computing <span class=\"m\">e^{x}</span> for large <span class=\"m\">x</span> directly, for example inside softmax. Subtract the maximum first.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Floating point stores numbers in binary scientific notation with fixed numbers of sign, exponent and fraction bits. Representable numbers are dense near zero and spread out for large magnitudes, so rounding errors are relative: at most <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon/2</span>, about <span class=\"m\">1.1 \\times 10^{-16}</span> in double. Most decimals like 0.1 are not exact, arithmetic is rounded at every step, and special values (<code>inf</code>, <code>NaN</code>, subnormals) handle the edges of the range.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"float\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A toy float with only a few fraction bits, so you can see every representable value.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start at 3 mantissa bits. Count the ticks between 1 and 2: there are <span class=\"m\">2^3 = 8</span> values, spaced <span class=\"m\">\\frac18</span> apart.\n2. Look between 2 and 4 and between 4 and 8. There are still 8 values in each, but the gaps are <span class=\"m\">\\frac14</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>.\n3. Drop to 1 mantissa bit, then raise to 5. Each extra bit doubles the number of values per power of two and halves every gap.\n4. Find where <span class=\"m\">6.5</span> would sit with 3 bits (<span class=\"m\">1.101_2 \\times 2^2</span>). It is exactly on a tick; <span class=\"m\">6.6</span> is not, and would round to <span class=\"m\">6.5</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the gap sizes double every time you cross a power of 2, so the gap divided by the number's size stays roughly constant. That is exactly the \"errors are relative\" rule from the article, and the extra mantissa bits are what double precision buys over single.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">x = (-1)^s \\times 1.f \\times 2^{e}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{stored exponent} = e + \\text{bias},\\quad \\text{bias} = 1023\\ (\\text{double}),\\ 127\\ (\\text{single})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\varepsilon_{\\text{double}} = 2^{-52} \\approx 2.22\\times10^{-16},\\quad \\varepsilon_{\\text{single}} = 2^{-23} \\approx 1.19\\times10^{-7}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{fl}(x) = x(1 + \\delta),\\quad |\\delta| \\le u = \\tfrac{\\varepsilon}{2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{spacing near } x \\approx \\varepsilon|x|</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Relative rounding error bound</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">x</span> lies in the normal range and <span class=\"m\">\\text{fl}(x)</span> is <span class=\"m\">x</span> rounded to the nearest floating-point number with <span class=\"m\">p</span> fraction bits, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{|\\text{fl}(x) - x|}{|x|} \\le 2^{-p-1} = u</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose <span class=\"m\">2^e \\le |x| &lt; 2^{e+1}</span>. In that range the floats are evenly spaced with gap <span class=\"m\">2^{e}\\cdot 2^{-p}</span>. Rounding to nearest moves <span class=\"m\">x</span> by at most half a gap, <span class=\"m\">2^{e-p-1}</span>. Dividing by <span class=\"m\">|x| \\ge 2^e</span> gives a relative error at most <span class=\"m\">2^{-p-1}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Encode −6.5 as a 32-bit single</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Binary: <span class=\"m\">6.5 = 110.1_2 = 1.101_2 \\times 2^2</span>.</li>\n<li>Sign bit 1 (negative). Exponent field <span class=\"m\">2 + 127 = 129 = 10000001_2</span>.</li>\n<li>Fraction field: <span class=\"m\">101</span> followed by 20 zeros.</li>\n<li>Bits: <code>1 10000001 10100000000000000000000</code>, which is <code>0xC0D00000</code> in hexadecimal.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Spacing of single-precision floats near 1000</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">512 = 2^9 \\le 1000 &lt; 2^{10}</span>, so the exponent is 9.</li>\n<li>Gap <span class=\"m\">= 2^9 \\times 2^{-23} = 2^{-14} \\approx 6.1 \\times 10^{-5}</span>.</li>\n<li>So a <code>float32</code> stores 1000 to about 4 decimal places; 1000.00001 rounds to 1000.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Why (1e16 + 1) − 1e16 is 0 in double precision</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">2^{53} \\approx 9.007 \\times 10^{15} \\le 10^{16} &lt; 2^{54}</span>, so the spacing there is <span class=\"m\">2^{53}\\cdot 2^{-52} = 2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">10^{16} + 1</span> is exactly halfway between two neighbours; ties round to the one with an even last bit, which is <span class=\"m\">10^{16}</span> itself.</li>\n<li>Subtracting <span class=\"m\">10^{16}</span> then gives 0, while <span class=\"m\">(10^{16} - 10^{16}) + 1 = 1</span>. Addition is not associative.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Half precision (<code>float16</code>) has 10 fraction bits. (a) What is the spacing between consecutive float16 numbers in <span class=\"m\">[512, 1024)</span> and in <span class=\"m\">[2048, 4096)</span>? (b) What does <span class=\"m\">2048 + 1</span> evaluate to in float16?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) The spacing in <span class=\"m\">[2^e, 2^{e+1})</span> is <span class=\"m\">2^{e-10}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">e = 9</span> it is <span class=\"m\">2^{-1} = 0.5</span>; for <span class=\"m\">e = 11</span> it is <span class=\"m\">2^{1} = 2</span>. (b) The neighbours of 2049 are 2048 and 2050, and 2049 is exactly halfway. Round-half-to-even picks the neighbour whose last fraction bit is 0, which is 2048. So <span class=\"m\">2048 + 1 = 2048</span> in float16. This is why integer counters must never be stored in half precision.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Mixed-precision training</strong> stores weights in float32 but does most arithmetic in float16 or <strong>bfloat16</strong> (1 sign, 8 exponent, 7 fraction bits). bfloat16 keeps float32's range so gradients rarely overflow, at the cost of only about 2 to 3 significant digits.</li>\n<li>float16 tops out at 65504 and loses tiny gradients to underflow, so training with it uses <strong>loss scaling</strong>: multiply the loss by a large constant before backpropagation and divide afterwards.</li>\n<li><strong>Quantization</strong> goes further, storing weights as 8-bit or 4-bit integers with a scale factor, trading precision for speed and memory.</li>\n<li>Softmax is computed as <span class=\"m\">e^{z_i - \\max z}/\\sum_j e^{z_j - \\max z}</span> so the exponentials never overflow.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Money should never be stored as binary floats, since <span class=\"m\">0.1 + 0.2 \\ne 0.3</span>. Financial systems use integer cents or decimal floating point. Database and JSON systems that pass 64-bit IDs through JavaScript (where every number is a double) silently corrupt IDs above <span class=\"m\">2^{53}</span>, which is why some APIs send large IDs as strings.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In 1991 a Patriot missile battery failed to intercept an incoming missile. Its clock counted time in tenths of a second, and 0.1 was stored in a 24-bit fixed-point register, which cut off the repeating binary expansion with an error of about <span class=\"m\">9.5 \\times 10^{-8}</span> seconds per tick. After 100 hours of continuous running the accumulated error was about 0.34 seconds, enough for the target to move over half a kilometre outside the tracking window.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many bits does an IEEE 754 double-precision number use?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"64\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits and 52 fraction bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In double precision, is 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 true?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 are all rounded; <span class=\"m\">0.1 + 0.2</span> gives <span class=\"m\">0.30000000000000004</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In a toy float with 3 fraction bits, what is the gap between consecutive numbers in [1, 2)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/8|0.125\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>There are <span class=\"m\">2^3 = 8</span> evenly spaced values per power of two, so the gap is <span class=\"m\">\\frac18</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In single precision, what is the stored (biased) exponent of 6.5 = 1.625 × 2²?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"129\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Bias 127 plus exponent 2.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which decimal number is stored exactly in binary floating point?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">0.1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">0.3</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">0.375</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0.375 = \\frac38 = 0.011_2</span> has a power-of-two denominator.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Roughly how many significant decimal digits does a double carry?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">7</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">16</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">32</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2^{-52} \\approx 2.2 \\times 10^{-16}</span>, so about 15 to 16 digits.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "error": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"every-computed-answer-is-approximate\" data-h=\"3\">Every computed answer is approximate</h3>\n<p>When a computer gives you a number, it is almost never exactly right. The input data may be measured with limited accuracy, the method may be an approximation like a truncated series, and every arithmetic step adds a tiny rounding error. Usually these errors are harmless. Sometimes they grow until the answer is meaningless. The skill this lesson teaches is to tell the two apart: to measure error properly, to recognise the operations that magnify it, and to separate problems that are inherently sensitive from algorithms that are just badly designed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine measuring two planks with a tape measure that is accurate to the nearest millimetre. Plank A is <span class=\"m\">2.000</span> m and plank B is <span class=\"m\">1.998</span> m, each uncertain by about <span class=\"m\">0.5</span> mm. The relative uncertainty of each measurement is tiny: <span class=\"m\">0.5</span> mm out of 2 m is <span class=\"m\">0.025\\%</span>. Now you need the <strong>difference</strong> in length: <span class=\"m\">2</span> mm. But each measurement could be off by <span class=\"m\">0.5</span> mm, so the difference could be anything from 1 mm to 3 mm, an uncertainty of <span class=\"m\">50\\%</span>. Nothing went wrong with the tape measure. Subtracting two nearly equal quantities simply cancels the digits you knew well and leaves the digits you didn't. This effect, <strong>catastrophic cancellation</strong>, is the most common cause of numerical disasters.</p>\n<h3 id=\"measuring-error\" data-h=\"3\">Measuring error</h3>\n<p>If the true value is <span class=\"m\">x</span> and the computed approximation is <span class=\"m\">\\hat x</span>, the <strong>absolute error</strong> is <span class=\"m\">|\\hat x - x|</span> and the <strong>relative error</strong> is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{|\\hat x - x|}{|x|}</span>. Approximating <span class=\"m\">\\pi</span> by <span class=\"m\">3.14</span> gives an absolute error of about <span class=\"m\">0.0016</span> and a relative error of about <span class=\"m\">0.0005</span>, or <span class=\"m\">0.05\\%</span>. An absolute error of 1 is tiny when measuring the distance to the Moon but huge when measuring a dose of medicine; relative error is usually the meaningful one. A relative error of about <span class=\"m\">10^{-k}</span> means the answer has about <span class=\"m\">k</span> correct significant digits.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Absolute and relative error</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a true value <span class=\"m\">x</span> and approximation <span class=\"m\">\\hat x</span>: absolute error <span class=\"m\">|\\hat x - x|</span>, relative error <span class=\"m\">|\\hat x - x|/|x|</span>. A relative error near <span class=\"m\">10^{-k}</span> means about <span class=\"m\">k</span> correct significant digits.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"where-error-comes-from\" data-h=\"3\">Where error comes from</h3>\n<p>There are four main sources. <strong>Modelling error</strong> comes from simplifying the real world, such as ignoring air resistance. <strong>Data error</strong> comes from measurements and earlier computations. <strong>Truncation error</strong> comes from replacing an infinite process with a finite one: approximating <span class=\"m\">e = 1 + 1 + \\frac12 + \\frac16 + \\frac1{24} + \\cdots</span> by its first four terms gives <span class=\"m\">2.6667</span>, a truncation error of about <span class=\"m\">0.0516</span>. <strong>Rounding error</strong> comes from storing every intermediate result in floating point. Truncation error shrinks as you do more work (more terms, smaller steps); rounding error usually grows with more work. Many numerical methods are a tug of war between the two.</p>\n<h3 id=\"catastrophic-cancellation\" data-h=\"3\">Catastrophic cancellation</h3>\n<p>Let's see cancellation with five-digit decimal arithmetic. Suppose the true values are <span class=\"m\">a = 1.23456</span> and <span class=\"m\">b = 1.23454</span>, so <span class=\"m\">a - b = 0.00002</span>. Rounded to five significant digits they become <span class=\"m\">1.2346</span> and <span class=\"m\">1.2345</span>, each with a relative error under <span class=\"m\">0.004\\%</span>. Their computed difference is <span class=\"m\">0.0001</span>, five times the truth: a relative error of <span class=\"m\">400\\%</span>. The subtraction itself was exact. It just exposed the earlier rounding, because the leading digits that agreed cancelled away and only the uncertain last digit remained.</p>\n<p>The cure is almost always algebra: rewrite the formula so the subtraction disappears. For large <span class=\"m\">x</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{x + 1} - \\sqrt{x}</span> subtracts two nearly equal numbers, but multiplying top and bottom by <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{x+1} + \\sqrt{x}</span> gives the equivalent <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + 1} + \\sqrt{x}}</span>, which only adds. At <span class=\"m\">x = 10^8</span> the first form loses about half of the 16 digits of a double; the second keeps them all. Similarly <span class=\"m\">1 - \\cos x</span> for small <span class=\"m\">x</span> should be computed as <span class=\"m\">2\\sin^2(x/2)</span>, and libraries provide <code>log1p(x)</code> <span class=\"m\">= \\ln(1 + x)</span> and <code>expm1(x)</code> <span class=\"m\">= e^x - 1</span> for small <span class=\"m\">x</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"conditioning-is-the-problem-itself-sensitive\" data-h=\"3\">Conditioning: is the problem itself sensitive?</h3>\n<p>Some problems amplify input errors no matter how you solve them. The <strong>condition number</strong> measures how much: it is the ratio of the relative change in output to the relative change in input. For a function of one variable,\n<span class=\"mb\">\\kappa(x) = \\left|\\frac{x f'(x)}{f(x)}\\right|.</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\sqrt x</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = \\left|\\frac{x\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x}}{\\sqrt x}\\right| = \\frac12</span>: square roots actually halve relative errors. For <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x - 1</span> near <span class=\"m\">x = 1.001</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = \\frac{1.001}{0.001} = 1001</span>: a <span class=\"m\">0.01\\%</span> error in <span class=\"m\">x</span> becomes about a <span class=\"m\">10\\%</span> error in the answer. That is the cancellation from the planks, measured. A rule of thumb: <strong>you can lose about <span class=\"m\">\\log_{10}\\kappa</span> significant digits</strong>. With <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = 10^6</span> and 16-digit doubles, expect about 10 correct digits.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Condition number</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>How strongly a problem amplifies relative input errors: <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = \\frac{\\text{relative change in output}}{\\text{relative change in input}}</span>, equal to <span class=\"m\">|x f'(x)/f(x)|</span> for a smooth function. Large <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span> means ill-conditioned.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"stability-is-the-algorithm-sensible\" data-h=\"3\">Stability: is the algorithm sensible?</h3>\n<p>Conditioning belongs to the problem; <strong>stability</strong> belongs to the algorithm. An algorithm is <strong>backward stable</strong> if its computed answer is the exact answer for a slightly perturbed input, perturbed by about machine epsilon. A backward stable algorithm on a well-conditioned problem gives an accurate answer. A backward stable algorithm on an ill-conditioned problem gives an answer with error about <span class=\"m\">\\kappa\\cdot u</span>, which is the best anyone can do. An unstable algorithm, like the naive quadratic formula for a tiny root, can ruin even a well-conditioned problem.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Stable algorithm</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An algorithm whose computed result is the exact result for input data perturbed by only a few units of roundoff (backward stability). Its error is then at most about the condition number times machine epsilon.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Blaming floating point for an ill-conditioned problem. If <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span> is <span class=\"m\">10^{12}</span>, no algorithm can give 16 digits.</li>\n<li>Blaming the problem for an unstable algorithm. The quadratic formula can be fixed by rearranging.</li>\n<li>Using absolute error to compare numbers of very different sizes.</li>\n<li>Computing a variance as <span class=\"m\">E[x^2] - (E[x])^2</span> with large means: two big nearly equal numbers cancel. Subtract the mean first.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Measure accuracy by relative error, which counts correct digits. Error comes from modelling, data, truncation and rounding. Subtracting nearly equal numbers cancels good digits and exposes bad ones, and is usually cured by rewriting the formula. The condition number <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = |xf'/f|</span> says how sensitive the problem is (lose about <span class=\"m\">\\log_{10}\\kappa</span> digits); stability says whether the algorithm adds damage of its own.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g07-approx\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;diff&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The error of a finite-difference derivative as the step h shrinks: truncation error falls, rounding error rises.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start at <span class=\"m\">h = 10^{-1}</span> and move left toward <span class=\"m\">10^{-5}</span>. The error falls steadily: this is truncation error shrinking.\n2. Keep going to <span class=\"m\">h = 10^{-12}</span> and beyond. The error starts rising again and becomes jagged: this is rounding error and cancellation in <span class=\"m\">f(x + h) - f(x)</span>.\n3. Find the lowest point of the orange (forward) curve, near <span class=\"m\">h \\approx 10^{-8}</span>, and of the purple (central) curve, near <span class=\"m\">h \\approx 10^{-5}</span>.\n4. At <span class=\"m\">h = 10^{-16}</span> the step is below machine epsilon relative to <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f(x + h) = f(x)</span> and the estimate collapses.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> this is the tug of war from the article in one picture. More work (smaller <span class=\"m\">h</span>) cuts truncation error but magnifies cancellation, so the best accuracy lies in the middle. The best forward error is only about <span class=\"m\">10^{-8}</span>, half the digits of a double, exactly as cancellation predicts.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{absolute error} = |\\hat x - x|,\\qquad \\text{relative error} = \\frac{|\\hat x - x|}{|x|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\kappa(x) = \\left|\\frac{x f'(x)}{f(x)}\\right|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{relative output error} \\approx \\kappa \\times \\text{relative input error}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sqrt{x + 1} - \\sqrt{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + 1} + \\sqrt{x}},\\qquad 1 - \\cos x = 2\\sin^2\\tfrac{x}{2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x_{1} = \\frac{-b - \\text{sign}(b)\\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a},\\quad x_2 = \\frac{c}{a x_1}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Error amplification by the condition number</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a differentiable <span class=\"m\">f</span> and a small relative input error <span class=\"m\">\\delta</span> (so the input is <span class=\"m\">x(1 + \\delta)</span>), the relative output error is approximately <span class=\"m\">\\kappa(x)\\,|\\delta|</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By Taylor's theorem, <span class=\"m\">f(x + x\\delta) \\approx f(x) + f'(x)x\\delta</span>. So\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{f(x + x\\delta) - f(x)}{f(x)} \\approx \\frac{x f'(x)}{f(x)}\\delta,</span>\nwhose absolute value is <span class=\"m\">\\kappa(x)|\\delta|</span>. The approximation ignores terms of order <span class=\"m\">\\delta^2</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Condition number of f(x) = x³ and of f(x) = ln x at x = 1.01</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^3</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = \\left|\\frac{x\\cdot3x^2}{x^3}\\right| = 3</span> everywhere. A 1% input error gives about 3% output error.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\ln x</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = \\left|\\frac{x\\cdot\\frac1x}{\\ln x}\\right| = \\frac{1}{|\\ln x|}</span>.</li>\n<li>At <span class=\"m\">x = 1.01</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\ln 1.01 \\approx 0.00995</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\kappa \\approx 100.5</span>. Logs near 1 are ill-conditioned; expect to lose about 2 digits.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">The small root of x² − 10⁸x + 1 = 0</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Roots: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{10^8 \\pm \\sqrt{10^{16} - 4}}{2}</span>. The large root is about <span class=\"m\">10^8</span>; the small one is about <span class=\"m\">10^{-8}</span>.</li>\n<li>Naive small root: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{10^8 - \\sqrt{10^{16} - 4}}{2}</span> subtracts two numbers that agree in about 16 digits. In double precision it returns about <span class=\"m\">7.45 \\times 10^{-9}</span>, a 25% error.</li>\n<li>Stable route: compute the large root <span class=\"m\">x_1 \\approx 10^8</span> without cancellation, then use the product of the roots <span class=\"m\">x_1 x_2 = c/a = 1</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1/x_1 \\approx 1.0 \\times 10^{-8}</span>, correct to full precision.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Relative error of an approximation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>True value 50, computed value 49.</li>\n<li>Absolute error <span class=\"m\">= 1</span>.</li>\n<li>Relative error <span class=\"m\">= 1/50 = 0.02 = 2\\%</span>, so about 2 significant digits are right.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) Compute the condition number of <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x - 1</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 1.0001</span>. (b) If <span class=\"m\">x</span> carries a relative error of <span class=\"m\">10^{-8}</span>, roughly how large is the relative error of <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span>? (c) Is this caused by the algorithm or by the problem?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = \\left|\\frac{x\\cdot1}{x - 1}\\right| = \\frac{1.0001}{0.0001} = 10001</span>. (b) About <span class=\"m\">10001 \\times 10^{-8} \\approx 10^{-4}</span>, so roughly 4 of the original 8 correct digits survive. (c) The problem: subtracting 1 from a number very close to 1 is ill-conditioned. The subtraction itself is computed exactly; it is the input's error that gets magnified.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Gradient checking</strong> compares backpropagated gradients with finite differences; the step must balance truncation and rounding, as in the widget.</li>\n<li><strong>Log-domain arithmetic:</strong> multiplying thousands of probabilities underflows to 0, so models add log-probabilities instead and use the log-sum-exp trick.</li>\n<li><strong>Variance and batch normalization:</strong> frameworks compute variance with the mean subtracted (or with Welford's algorithm) to avoid cancellation, and add a small <span class=\"m\">\\epsilon</span> before dividing by a standard deviation.</li>\n<li><strong>Ill-conditioned optimization:</strong> a loss surface whose curvature differs hugely between directions has a large condition number, which slows gradient descent.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Summing many numbers accumulates rounding error proportional to the count. <strong>Kahan summation</strong> keeps a running compensation term that captures the low-order bits lost at each step, making the error essentially independent of the number of terms. Python's <code>math.fsum</code> goes further and returns the correctly rounded sum.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In 1982 the Vancouver Stock Exchange started a new index at 1000. After each of thousands of daily trades it was recomputed and <strong>truncated</strong> (not rounded) to three decimal places. The tiny downward bias of each truncation accumulated, and 22 months later the index read about 524.8. Recomputed correctly, the value was about 1098.9.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">True value 200, computed value 202. What is the relative error?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.01|1/100\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|202 - 200|/200 = 0.01</span>, or 1%.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Condition number of f(x) = √x?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/2|0.5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|x\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x}/\\sqrt x| = \\frac12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Condition number of f(x) = x³?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|x\\cdot3x^2/x^3| = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With κ = 10⁶ and double precision (about 16 digits), roughly how many correct digits should you expect?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>You lose about <span class=\"m\">\\log_{10}\\kappa = 6</span> digits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which operation causes catastrophic cancellation?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Multiplying two large numbers</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Subtracting two nearly equal numbers</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Adding two positive numbers</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Leading digits cancel and only the uncertain trailing digits remain.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A backward stable algorithm applied to an ill-conditioned problem gives:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">A fully accurate answer</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">An answer with error about κ times machine epsilon</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Always NaN</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Stability limits the algorithm's damage, but the problem's sensitivity remains.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "root-finding": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-need-numerical-root-finding\" data-h=\"3\">Why we need numerical root finding</h3>\n<p>Many questions in science and engineering boil down to \"for which <span class=\"m\">x</span> is <span class=\"m\">f(x) = 0</span>?\". At what interest rate does an investment break even? At what angle does a projectile hit the target? Where is the gradient of a loss zero? For a quadratic there is a formula, but for something as simple as <span class=\"m\">\\cos x = x</span> or <span class=\"m\">x^5 - x - 1 = 0</span> there is none (and the Abel–Ruffini theorem proves no general formula exists for degree five and above). Instead we use <strong>iterative methods</strong>: start with a guess and improve it step by step until it is good enough. This lesson covers the three classic methods and, just as important, how quickly each one homes in on the answer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine guessing a number between 1 and 100 where the only feedback is \"too high\" or \"too low\". The smart strategy is to guess the middle, 50, and throw away the half that can't contain the answer. After 7 guesses you are certain, since <span class=\"m\">2^7 = 128 &gt; 100</span>. That is the <strong>bisection method</strong>. Now imagine that instead of \"too high\" you are told <em>how far</em> off you are and in which direction the error is changing. You could jump straight toward the answer instead of halving. That is the idea behind <strong>Newton's method</strong>, and it is spectacularly faster when it works.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-bisection-method\" data-h=\"3\">The bisection method</h3>\n<p>Bisection relies on the <strong>intermediate value theorem</strong>: if <span class=\"m\">f</span> is continuous and <span class=\"m\">f(a)</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(b)</span> have opposite signs, there is a root between <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span>. So pick such a bracket, evaluate <span class=\"m\">f</span> at the midpoint <span class=\"m\">m = \\frac{a + b}{2}</span>, and keep whichever half still has a sign change. Let's find <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt2</span> as the root of <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2 - 2</span> on <span class=\"m\">[1, 2]</span>, where <span class=\"m\">f(1) = -1 &lt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(2) = 2 &gt; 0</span>:</p>\n<p>At <span class=\"m\">m = 1.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">f = 0.25 &gt; 0</span>, so the root is in <span class=\"m\">[1, 1.5]</span>. At <span class=\"m\">m = 1.25</span>, <span class=\"m\">f = -0.4375 &lt; 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">[1.25, 1.5]</span>. At <span class=\"m\">m = 1.375</span>, <span class=\"m\">f \\approx -0.109</span>, so <span class=\"m\">[1.375, 1.5]</span>. At <span class=\"m\">m = 1.4375</span>, <span class=\"m\">f \\approx 0.066</span>, so <span class=\"m\">[1.375, 1.4375]</span>. The true root <span class=\"m\">1.41421\\ldots</span> is trapped in an interval of width <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{16}</span>.</p>\n<p>Each step halves the bracket, so after <span class=\"m\">n</span> steps the error is at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac{b - a}{2^{n}}</span> (or half that if you report the midpoint). To get from width 1 to <span class=\"m\">10^{-6}</span> takes 20 steps, since <span class=\"m\">2^{20} \\approx 10^6</span>. Bisection is slow but <strong>guaranteed</strong>: it can't diverge, and it needs only the sign of <span class=\"m\">f</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bisection method</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Repeatedly halve an interval <span class=\"m\">[a, b]</span> on which <span class=\"m\">f</span> changes sign, keeping the half where the sign change remains. After <span class=\"m\">n</span> steps the root is located within <span class=\"m\">(b - a)/2^n</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"newton-s-method\" data-h=\"3\">Newton's method</h3>\n<p>Newton's method uses the derivative. Near the current guess <span class=\"m\">x_n</span>, the curve looks like its tangent line <span class=\"m\">y = f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n)</span>. Instead of solving the hard equation <span class=\"m\">f(x) = 0</span>, solve the easy linear one: set the tangent to zero and get\n<span class=\"mb\">x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2 - 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 2x</span>, and the update simplifies to <span class=\"m\">x_{n+1} = \\frac12\\left(x_n + \\frac{2}{x_n}\\right)</span>, the ancient Babylonian square-root method. Starting from <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 1.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_2 \\approx 1.416667</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_3 \\approx 1.414215686</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_4 \\approx 1.41421356237469</span>. The number of correct digits goes 1, 3, 6, 12: it roughly <strong>doubles</strong> every step. That is <strong>quadratic convergence</strong>: the new error is about a constant times the square of the old error, <span class=\"m\">e_{n+1} \\approx C e_n^2</span>. An error of <span class=\"m\">10^{-3}</span> becomes about <span class=\"m\">10^{-6}</span>, then <span class=\"m\">10^{-12}</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Newton's method</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The iteration <span class=\"m\">x_{n+1} = x_n - f(x_n)/f'(x_n)</span>, which jumps to the root of the tangent line at the current guess. Near a simple root it converges quadratically.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"when-newton-goes-wrong\" data-h=\"3\">When Newton goes wrong</h3>\n<p>Newton's speed comes with no guarantee. If <span class=\"m\">f'(x_n)</span> is zero or tiny, the tangent is flat and the next guess flies off to infinity. If the starting point is far from the root, the iteration can wander or even cycle: for <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^3 - 2x + 2</span> starting at <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 0</span>, we get <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 0 - \\frac{2}{-2} = 1</span> and then <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1 - \\frac{1}{1} = 0</span>, bouncing between 0 and 1 forever. At a <strong>double root</strong>, where <span class=\"m\">f'(r) = 0</span> too, convergence drops to linear. Robust software therefore combines methods: it keeps a bisection bracket as a safety net and takes Newton or secant steps when they stay inside it, as in Brent's method.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-secant-method\" data-h=\"3\">The secant method</h3>\n<p>Often the derivative is unavailable or expensive. The <strong>secant method</strong> replaces the tangent with the line through the last two points:\n<span class=\"mb\">x_{n+1} = x_n - f(x_n)\\frac{x_n - x_{n-1}}{f(x_n) - f(x_{n-1})}.</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">x^2 - 2</span> with <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 2 - 2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2 - (-1)} = \\frac43 \\approx 1.333</span>, then <span class=\"m\">x_3 = 1.4</span>. Its convergence order is the golden ratio, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1 + \\sqrt5}{2} \\approx 1.618</span>: slower than Newton per step, but each step needs only one new function value, so it is often faster in total time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"stopping-and-measuring-convergence\" data-h=\"3\">Stopping and measuring convergence</h3>\n<p>In practice we stop when the step <span class=\"m\">|x_{n+1} - x_n|</span> or the residual <span class=\"m\">|f(x_n)|</span> falls below a tolerance, and also cap the number of iterations. A method has <strong>order</strong> <span class=\"m\">p</span> if <span class=\"m\">|e_{n+1}| \\approx C|e_n|^p</span>: <span class=\"m\">p = 1</span> is linear (bisection, with <span class=\"m\">C = \\frac12</span>), <span class=\"m\">p \\approx 1.618</span> is the secant method, and <span class=\"m\">p = 2</span> is Newton.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Using bisection without first checking that <span class=\"m\">f(a)</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(b)</span> have opposite signs.</li>\n<li>Trusting Newton from any starting point. Check the derivative isn't near zero and that the residual is actually shrinking.</li>\n<li>Stopping only when <span class=\"m\">|f(x)|</span> is small: for a very flat function <span class=\"m\">|f|</span> can be tiny far from the root.</li>\n<li>Expecting bisection to find roots where <span class=\"m\">f</span> touches zero without crossing, like <span class=\"m\">x^2 = 0</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Bisection halves a sign-change bracket each step: slow (linear, one bit per step) but guaranteed. Newton's method follows tangent lines, <span class=\"m\">x_{n+1} = x_n - f/f'</span>, doubling the correct digits each step near a simple root but able to fail far from it. The secant method replaces the derivative with a difference quotient and converges with order about 1.618. Practical solvers combine a safe bracket with fast steps.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"newton\" data-args='{&quot;x0&quot;: 1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Each Step jumps to where the tangent line crosses zero.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2 - 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 1</span>, press Step four times and compare the iterates with the article: 1.5, 1.41667, 1.414216, 1.41421356.\n2. Switch to <span class=\"m\">x^3 - 2x - 5</span> (Newton's own 1669 example) and step from the default start. The root is about 2.0946.\n3. Switch to <span class=\"m\">x^3 - 2x + 2</span> and start at <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 0</span>. Watch the iterates bounce between 0 and 1: the cycle from the article.\n4. Try <span class=\"m\">\\cos x - x</span> and count how many steps it takes to settle near 0.7391.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> when Newton works, the error column drops like <span class=\"m\">10^{-1}, 10^{-3}, 10^{-6}, 10^{-12}</span>, doubling the digits. When it fails, the tangent line points somewhere unhelpful. Speed and safety are the trade-off every root finder balances.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Bisection: } |x_n - r| \\le \\frac{b - a}{2^{n+1}}\\ \\text{(midpoint after } n \\text{ halvings)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Newton: } x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Secant: } x_{n+1} = x_n - f(x_n)\\frac{x_n - x_{n-1}}{f(x_n) - f(x_{n-1})}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Order } p:\\ |e_{n+1}| \\approx C|e_n|^p;\\quad p = 1,\\ 1.618,\\ 2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Newton error: } e_{n+1} \\approx \\frac{f''(r)}{2f'(r)}e_n^2</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Convergence of bisection</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is continuous on <span class=\"m\">[a, b]</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(a)f(b) &lt; 0</span>, bisection's midpoints converge to a root <span class=\"m\">r</span>, with <span class=\"m\">|m_n - r| \\le (b - a)/2^{n+1}</span> after <span class=\"m\">n</span> halvings.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By the intermediate value theorem each bracket contains a root. Each step keeps a half with a sign change, so after <span class=\"m\">n</span> steps the bracket has width <span class=\"m\">(b - a)/2^n</span> and contains a root; its midpoint is within half the width, <span class=\"m\">(b - a)/2^{n+1}</span>, of that root. The width tends to 0, so the midpoints converge.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Quadratic convergence of Newton's method</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is twice continuously differentiable, <span class=\"m\">f(r) = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f'(r) \\ne 0</span>, then for <span class=\"m\">x_0</span> close enough to <span class=\"m\">r</span> the Newton errors satisfy <span class=\"m\">e_{n+1} \\approx \\frac{f''(r)}{2f'(r)}e_n^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">e_n = x_n - r</span>. Taylor expand about <span class=\"m\">x_n</span>: <span class=\"m\">0 = f(r) = f(x_n) - f'(x_n)e_n + \\frac12 f''(\\xi)e_n^2</span> for some <span class=\"m\">\\xi</span> between <span class=\"m\">r</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_n</span>. Divide by <span class=\"m\">f'(x_n)</span> and rearrange: <span class=\"m\">x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)} - r = \\frac{f''(\\xi)}{2f'(x_n)}e_n^2</span>. The left side is <span class=\"m\">x_{n+1} - r = e_{n+1}</span>, and as <span class=\"m\">x_n \\to r</span> the factor tends to <span class=\"m\">\\frac{f''(r)}{2f'(r)}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">One Newton step for x³ − 2x − 5 = 0 from x₀ = 2</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(2) = 8 - 4 - 5 = -1</span> and <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 3x^2 - 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f'(2) = 10</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x_1 = 2 - \\frac{-1}{10} = 2.1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(2.1) = 9.261 - 4.2 - 5 = 0.061</span>, <span class=\"m\">f'(2.1) = 11.23</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 2.1 - 0.00543 \\approx 2.09457</span>. The true root is <span class=\"m\">2.094551\\ldots</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many bisection steps for 10⁻⁶ accuracy on [0, 1]?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>After <span class=\"m\">n</span> halvings the midpoint error is at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{2^{n+1}}</span>.</li>\n<li>Need <span class=\"m\">2^{n+1} \\ge 10^6</span>; since <span class=\"m\">2^{20} = 1{,}048{,}576</span>, <span class=\"m\">n + 1 = 20</span> works.</li>\n<li>So 19 halvings suffice for the midpoint (20 if you require the whole bracket to be that narrow). Newton from a good start would need about 4 or 5 steps.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Two secant steps for x² − 2 from x₀ = 1, x₁ = 2</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(1) = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(2) = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 2 - 2\\cdot\\frac{2 - 1}{2 - (-1)} = \\frac43</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(\\frac43) = \\frac{16}{9} - 2 = -\\frac29</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_3 = \\frac43 - \\left(-\\frac29\\right)\\frac{\\frac43 - 2}{-\\frac29 - 2} = \\frac43 + \\frac{1}{15} = 1.4</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Computers can find <span class=\"m\">1/a</span> without division by applying Newton's method to <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\frac1x - a</span>. (a) Show that the update is <span class=\"m\">x_{n+1} = x_n(2 - a x_n)</span>. (b) With <span class=\"m\">a = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 0.3</span>, compute <span class=\"m\">x_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_2</span>. (c) Show that <span class=\"m\">1 - a x_{n+1} = (1 - a x_n)^2</span> and explain what this says about convergence.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = -\\frac1{x^2}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{1/x_n - a}{-1/x_n^2} = x_n + x_n - a x_n^2 = x_n(2 - a x_n)</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 0.3(2 - 0.9) = 0.33</span>; <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 0.33(2 - 0.99) = 0.3333</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">1 - a x_n(2 - a x_n) = 1 - 2ax_n + a^2x_n^2 = (1 - ax_n)^2</span>. The relative error is squared each step: <span class=\"m\">0.1, 0.01, 0.0001, 10^{-8}</span>. That is quadratic convergence, and it works whenever <span class=\"m\">0 &lt; x_0 &lt; 2/a</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Second-order optimization:</strong> a minimum of a loss <span class=\"m\">L</span> is a root of <span class=\"m\">\\nabla L</span>, and Newton's method for it is <span class=\"m\">\\theta \\leftarrow \\theta - H^{-1}\\nabla L</span>, with <span class=\"m\">H</span> the Hessian. Logistic regression is classically fitted this way (iteratively reweighted least squares).</li>\n<li><strong>Line search</strong> in optimizers like L-BFGS solves a one-dimensional root or minimum problem at every step.</li>\n<li><strong>Implicit layers and equilibrium models</strong> find the output by solving <span class=\"m\">z = g(z, x)</span> with Newton-type or fixed-point solvers.</li>\n<li><strong>Inverse CDFs</strong> for sampling and quantiles are often computed by Newton's method on <span class=\"m\">F(x) - u = 0</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Hardware and math libraries compute square roots and reciprocals with a cheap initial estimate followed by one or two Newton steps. The famous \"fast inverse square root\" in the game Quake III used a bit-level trick for the first guess and then one Newton step for <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt{x}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The <strong>internal rate of return</strong> of an investment is the interest rate <span class=\"m\">r</span> that makes the net present value zero: <span class=\"m\">\\sum_t \\frac{C_t}{(1 + r)^t} = 0</span>. There is no formula for it; spreadsheet functions like <code>IRR</code> find it with Newton or secant iterations from a starting guess, and occasionally fail to converge for exactly the reasons in this lesson.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">One Newton step for f(x) = x² − 9 from x₀ = 5?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3.4|17/5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">5 - \\frac{25 - 9}{10} = 5 - 1.6 = 3.4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">One Newton step for x² − 2 from x₀ = 1?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.5|3/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 - \\frac{-1}{2} = 1.5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Bisection starts with an interval of width 1. What is the bracket width after 10 steps?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/1024|0.0009765625\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each step halves it: <span class=\"m\">2^{-10} = \\frac{1}{1024}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does bisection require to start?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">A continuous function with f(a) and f(b) of opposite signs</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">The derivative of f</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">A starting point near the root</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The intermediate value theorem then guarantees a root in the bracket.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Near a simple root, Newton's method roughly:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Adds one correct digit per step</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Doubles the number of correct digits per step</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Halves the error per step</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Quadratic convergence: <span class=\"m\">e_{n+1} \\approx Ce_n^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The convergence order of the secant method is about:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">1.618</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">3</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The golden ratio <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1 + \\sqrt5}{2}</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "interpolation": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"filling-in-between-the-data\" data-h=\"3\">Filling in between the data</h3>\n<p>Data usually arrives as points: a temperature reading every hour, a value of <span class=\"m\">\\sin x</span> from a printed table, a few frames of an animation, a handful of expensive simulation results. Often we need values <strong>between</strong> the points. <strong>Interpolation</strong> builds a function that passes exactly through every given point and uses it to estimate the values in between. It is the foundation of numerical integration and differentiation, of the curves in fonts and graphics, of image resizing and of audio resampling. It also hides a famous trap: adding more points to a polynomial can make it <em>worse</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a printed table that gives <span class=\"m\">\\sin 0.5 = 0.4794</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sin 0.6 = 0.5646</span>, and you need <span class=\"m\">\\sin 0.55</span>. The simplest idea is to draw a straight line between the two points and read off the middle: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.4794 + 0.5646}{2} = 0.5220</span>. The true value is <span class=\"m\">0.52269</span>, so the straight line is off by about <span class=\"m\">0.0007</span>. Before calculators, people did exactly this with books of tables. Can we do better? With three points we can fit a parabola, which bends like the real curve; with more points, a higher-degree polynomial.</p>\n<h3 id=\"linear-interpolation\" data-h=\"3\">Linear interpolation</h3>\n<p>Through two points <span class=\"m\">(x_0, y_0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(x_1, y_1)</span> there is exactly one line:\n<span class=\"mb\">p(x) = y_0 + \\frac{y_1 - y_0}{x_1 - x_0}(x - x_0).</span>\nFor points <span class=\"m\">(2, 10)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(4, 20)</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(3) = 10 + 5\\cdot1 = 15</span>. Linear interpolation is exact for straight lines and has error at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac{h^2}{8}\\max|f''|</span> for a smooth function with spacing <span class=\"m\">h</span>. For the sine table, <span class=\"m\">h = 0.1</span> and <span class=\"m\">|f''| = |\\sin x| \\le 0.565</span> on that interval, so the error is at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.01}{8}(0.565) \\approx 0.0007</span>, exactly what we saw.</p>\n<h3 id=\"polynomial-interpolation\" data-h=\"3\">Polynomial interpolation</h3>\n<p>Given <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> points with distinct <span class=\"m\">x</span>-values, there is <strong>exactly one</strong> polynomial of degree at most <span class=\"m\">n</span> through all of them. For the points <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(2, 7)</span> it is <span class=\"m\">p(x) = x^2 + x + 1</span>: check <span class=\"m\">p(0) = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(1) = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(2) = 7</span>. There are several ways to write the same polynomial.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Lagrange form</strong> builds it from basis polynomials <span class=\"m\">L_i(x) = \\prod_{j \\ne i}\\frac{x - x_j}{x_i - x_j}</span>, each equal to 1 at its own node <span class=\"m\">x_i</span> and 0 at every other node. Then <span class=\"m\">p(x) = \\sum_i y_i L_i(x)</span> obviously passes through every point.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Newton form</strong> builds it one point at a time with <strong>divided differences</strong>. For our three points: <span class=\"m\">f[x_0] = 1</span>; <span class=\"m\">f[x_0, x_1] = \\frac{3 - 1}{1 - 0} = 2</span>; <span class=\"m\">f[x_1, x_2] = \\frac{7 - 3}{2 - 1} = 4</span>; and <span class=\"m\">f[x_0, x_1, x_2] = \\frac{4 - 2}{2 - 0} = 1</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">p(x) = 1 + 2(x - 0) + 1(x - 0)(x - 1) = x^2 + x + 1</span>, the same polynomial. Adding a fourth point just adds one more term, which is why the Newton form is convenient in practice.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Interpolating polynomial</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> points <span class=\"m\">(x_i, y_i)</span> with distinct <span class=\"m\">x_i</span>, the unique polynomial <span class=\"m\">p</span> of degree at most <span class=\"m\">n</span> with <span class=\"m\">p(x_i) = y_i</span> for every <span class=\"m\">i</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Divided differences</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">f[x_i] = y_i</span> and <span class=\"m\">f[x_i, \\ldots, x_{i+k}] = \\frac{f[x_{i+1}, \\ldots, x_{i+k}] - f[x_i, \\ldots, x_{i+k-1}]}{x_{i+k} - x_i}</span>; they are the coefficients of the Newton form of the interpolating polynomial.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-big-is-the-error\" data-h=\"3\">How big is the error?</h3>\n<p>If the data come from a smooth function <span class=\"m\">f</span>, the error of the interpolating polynomial is\n<span class=\"mb\">f(x) - p(x) = \\frac{f^{(n+1)}(\\xi)}{(n+1)!}\\prod_{i=0}^{n}(x - x_i)</span>\nfor some <span class=\"m\">\\xi</span> in the range of the points. Two things control it: how wild the <span class=\"m\">(n+1)</span>st derivative of <span class=\"m\">f</span> is, and the size of the <strong>node polynomial</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\prod(x - x_i)</span>, which depends only on where you put the points. Between closely packed nodes the product is small; near the ends of an evenly spaced grid it becomes large.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-runge-phenomenon\" data-h=\"3\">The Runge phenomenon</h3>\n<p>In 1901 Carl Runge tried interpolating the innocent-looking bell <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\frac{1}{1 + 25x^2}</span> on <span class=\"m\">[-1, 1]</span> with evenly spaced points. With 5 points the fit is rough. With 11 points it fits the middle beautifully but swings wildly near the ends, overshooting to almost 2. With 21 points the swings are in the dozens. <strong>More data made the fit worse</strong>, because the derivatives of this <span class=\"m\">f</span> grow very fast and the node polynomial is huge near the ends.</p>\n<p>There are two cures. First, move the points: <strong>Chebyshev nodes</strong>, <span class=\"m\">x_i = \\cos\\frac{(2i + 1)\\pi}{2(n + 1)}</span>, cluster toward the ends of the interval, which keeps the node polynomial as small as possible everywhere. With them, the error for this function shrinks steadily as <span class=\"m\">n</span> grows. Second, stop using one high-degree polynomial: a <strong>spline</strong> joins low-degree pieces.</p>\n<h3 id=\"splines\" data-h=\"3\">Splines</h3>\n<p>A <strong>cubic spline</strong> uses a separate cubic polynomial on each interval between neighbouring points, chosen so that the whole curve passes through all the points and has continuous first and second derivatives at the joins. The result looks smooth to the eye and doesn't oscillate, because each piece only has to bend a little. Adding points makes a spline better, not worse. Splines draw the letters on your screen, the curves in vector graphics tools and the paths of animated objects.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cubic spline</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A piecewise cubic function through the data points whose value, first derivative and second derivative are continuous at every interior point.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Assuming a higher-degree polynomial is always more accurate. With evenly spaced points it can diverge (Runge).</li>\n<li><strong>Extrapolating</strong> far outside the data. Polynomials grow rapidly outside the range of the nodes.</li>\n<li>Solving for polynomial coefficients through the Vandermonde matrix with many points: that system is badly ill-conditioned. Use Newton, barycentric Lagrange or Chebyshev forms.</li>\n<li>Confusing interpolation (pass through every point) with regression (fit a trend through noisy points).</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Interpolation builds a function through given points to estimate values between them. Through <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> points there is exactly one polynomial of degree at most <span class=\"m\">n</span>, written in Lagrange or Newton form. Its error depends on the function's derivatives and on the node placement; evenly spaced high-degree interpolation can oscillate wildly (Runge's phenomenon), while Chebyshev nodes or piecewise cubic splines stay accurate.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g07-approx\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;interp&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Interpolate Runge's function with evenly spaced points, Chebyshev points or a cubic spline.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <strong>Evenly spaced polynomial</strong>, set the degree to 4, then 10, then 20. Watch the max error grow and the ends swing wildly: the Runge phenomenon.\n2. Switch to <strong>Chebyshev nodes polynomial</strong> at degree 20. The pink points crowd toward the ends and the max error is now small.\n3. Switch to <strong>Cubic spline (even)</strong> with the same evenly spaced points. The curve hugs the bell with no wild swings.\n4. Compare the max error readouts of all three at degree 10.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the function and the number of points never changed, only where the points sit or what kind of curve joins them. That is the error formula at work: node placement controls <span class=\"m\">\\prod(x - x_i)</span>, and splines avoid high degrees altogether.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"bias-variance\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The statistical cousin: a high-degree polynomial fitted to noisy points.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Raise the degree from 3 to 15 and watch the curve chase the noisy points.\n2. Compare the training and test errors at high degree.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> high-degree polynomials wiggle both in interpolation (Runge) and in regression (overfitting). In both cases flexibility without control hurts accuracy away from the data points.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">p(x) = y_0 + \\frac{y_1 - y_0}{x_1 - x_0}(x - x_0)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p(x) = \\sum_{i=0}^n y_i L_i(x),\\quad L_i(x) = \\prod_{j \\ne i}\\frac{x - x_j}{x_i - x_j}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p(x) = f[x_0] + f[x_0, x_1](x - x_0) + f[x_0, x_1, x_2](x - x_0)(x - x_1) + \\cdots</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(x) - p(x) = \\frac{f^{(n+1)}(\\xi)}{(n + 1)!}\\prod_{i=0}^n(x - x_i)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Chebyshev nodes: } x_i = \\cos\\frac{(2i + 1)\\pi}{2(n + 1)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Existence and uniqueness of the interpolating polynomial</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> points with distinct <span class=\"m\">x_0, \\ldots, x_n</span> there is exactly one polynomial of degree at most <span class=\"m\">n</span> through all of them.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Existence: the Lagrange form <span class=\"m\">\\sum y_i L_i(x)</span> has degree at most <span class=\"m\">n</span> and satisfies <span class=\"m\">p(x_k) = y_k</span> because <span class=\"m\">L_i(x_k)</span> is 1 if <span class=\"m\">i = k</span> and 0 otherwise. Uniqueness: if <span class=\"m\">p</span> and <span class=\"m\">q</span> both interpolate, then <span class=\"m\">p - q</span> has degree at most <span class=\"m\">n</span> and vanishes at <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> distinct points. A nonzero polynomial of degree at most <span class=\"m\">n</span> has at most <span class=\"m\">n</span> roots, so <span class=\"m\">p - q = 0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Interpolate (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 7) with divided differences and evaluate at x = 3</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>First differences: <span class=\"m\">f[0, 1] = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f[1, 2] = 4</span>.</li>\n<li>Second difference: <span class=\"m\">f[0, 1, 2] = \\frac{4 - 2}{2 - 0} = 1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">p(x) = 1 + 2x + x(x - 1) = x^2 + x + 1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">p(3) = 9 + 3 + 1 = 13</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Linear interpolation for sin 0.55</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Points <span class=\"m\">(0.5, 0.4794)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0.6, 0.5646)</span>; <span class=\"m\">x = 0.55</span> is the midpoint.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">p(0.55) = 0.4794 + \\frac{0.0852}{0.1}(0.05) = 0.5220</span>.</li>\n<li>True value <span class=\"m\">0.52269</span>; error <span class=\"m\">\\approx 0.0007</span>, matching the bound <span class=\"m\">\\frac{h^2}{8}\\max|f''|</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Interpolate <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\frac1x</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 1, 2, 4</span> with a quadratic. (a) Find the Newton form. (b) Estimate <span class=\"m\">f(3)</span> and compare with the true value. (c) Use the error formula to explain the sign of the error.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Values <span class=\"m\">1, \\frac12, \\frac14</span>. <span class=\"m\">f[1, 2] = \\frac{0.5 - 1}{1} = -0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">f[2, 4] = \\frac{0.25 - 0.5}{2} = -0.125</span>, <span class=\"m\">f[1, 2, 4] = \\frac{-0.125 + 0.5}{3} = 0.125</span>. So <span class=\"m\">p(x) = 1 - 0.5(x - 1) + 0.125(x - 1)(x - 2)</span>.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">p(3) = 1 - 1 + 0.125(2)(1) = 0.25</span>, while <span class=\"m\">f(3) = \\frac13 \\approx 0.333</span>, an error of about <span class=\"m\">0.083</span>.\n(c) <span class=\"m\">f'''(x) = -\\frac{6}{x^4}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f(3) - p(3) = \\frac{-6/\\xi^4}{6}(3 - 1)(3 - 2)(3 - 4) = \\frac{2}{\\xi^4} &gt; 0</span>. The interpolant underestimates, as observed (the value <span class=\"m\">\\xi \\approx 2.21</span> makes it exact).</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Learning-rate schedules and lookup tables</strong> are often piecewise-linear interpolations between a few set values.</li>\n<li><strong>Image resizing</strong> in data augmentation uses bilinear or bicubic interpolation; upsampling layers in neural networks use the same idea.</li>\n<li><strong>Positional embeddings</strong> in vision transformers are interpolated when the model is run at a new image resolution.</li>\n<li>The Runge phenomenon is the deterministic mirror of <strong>overfitting</strong>: perfect agreement at the data points, wild behaviour between them.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Fonts (TrueType and PostScript) and vector graphics store shapes as quadratic or cubic Bézier and spline curves. Audio sample-rate conversion and video frame interpolation are interpolation problems, and the Fast Fourier Transform is closely related to interpolation by trigonometric polynomials.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Weather maps show smooth temperature contours even though stations are scattered; the values between stations are interpolated. Animators set a few key frames and let software interpolate the motion between them with splines (\"tweening\").</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Through how many points does a unique polynomial of degree at most 3 pass?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> points determine a polynomial of degree at most <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Linear interpolation between (2, 10) and (4, 20) at x = 3?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"15\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The midpoint of the line: <span class=\"m\">10 + 5 = 15</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The quadratic through (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 7) is x² + x + 1. What is p(3)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"13\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">9 + 3 + 1 = 13</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does the Runge phenomenon describe?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Splines failing at the ends</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">High-degree interpolation on evenly spaced points oscillating wildly near the ends</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Linear interpolation being exact</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Seen with <span class=\"m\">1/(1 + 25x^2)</span> on <span class=\"m\">[-1, 1]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which fixes the Runge phenomenon?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Using more evenly spaced points</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Using Chebyshev nodes or splines</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Extrapolating instead</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Chebyshev nodes cluster near the ends; splines use low-degree pieces.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Divided difference f[x₀, x₁] for points (1, 4) and (3, 10)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{10 - 4}{3 - 1} = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "num-calculus": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"derivatives-without-a-formula\" data-h=\"3\">Derivatives without a formula</h3>\n<p>Calculus teaches us to differentiate formulas, but computers often face functions without a usable formula: a physics simulation, a black-box program, a measured signal, or a loss computed by thousands of lines of code. We can still evaluate such a function at any point we like. <strong>Numerical differentiation</strong> estimates the derivative from a few of those values. It is the oldest trick in the book (it is just the definition of the derivative with a small, finite step) and it has a surprising twist: making the step smaller only helps up to a point.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you are in a car with a broken speedometer but a working odometer. At 2:00:00 the odometer reads 100.000 km and at 2:00:10 it reads 100.250 km. Your average speed over those 10 seconds was <span class=\"m\">0.25</span> km per 10 s, which is 90 km/h. That is a <strong>finite difference</strong>: change in distance divided by change in time. If the car was accelerating, 90 km/h is not exactly your speed at 2:00:00. Using a shorter interval would be more accurate, but if the odometer only shows three decimal places, a 0.1-second interval would show a change of 0.002 or 0.003 km, and rounding would ruin the estimate. Shorter steps reduce one error and amplify another. That exact tension is the heart of this lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-forward-difference\" data-h=\"3\">The forward difference</h3>\n<p>The derivative is defined as the limit <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h}</span>. If we stop before the limit and use a small but finite <span class=\"m\">h</span>, we get the <strong>forward difference</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_+ f(x) = \\frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h}.</span>\nHow wrong is it? Taylor's theorem says <span class=\"m\">f(x + h) = f(x) + hf'(x) + \\frac{h^2}{2}f''(\\xi)</span> for some <span class=\"m\">\\xi</span> between <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">x + h</span>. Substituting,\n<span class=\"mb\">D_+ f(x) = f'(x) + \\frac{h}{2}f''(\\xi).</span>\nSo the <strong>truncation error</strong> is about <span class=\"m\">\\frac h2 f''</span>: it is proportional to <span class=\"m\">h</span>. We say the method is <strong>first order</strong>. Halve <span class=\"m\">h</span> and the error halves. For <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span> with <span class=\"m\">h = 0.1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{9.61 - 9}{0.1} = 6.1</span>, while the true derivative is 6. The error is <span class=\"m\">0.1</span>, exactly <span class=\"m\">\\frac h2 f'' = \\frac{0.1}{2}\\cdot 2</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Finite difference</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An approximation of a derivative by a difference quotient of function values at nearby points, such as <span class=\"m\">\\frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-central-difference\" data-h=\"3\">The central difference</h3>\n<p>Now look both ways. Using one point on each side, the <strong>central difference</strong> is\n<span class=\"mb\">D_0 f(x) = \\frac{f(x + h) - f(x - h)}{2h}.</span>\nExpanding both values with Taylor's theorem, the <span class=\"m\">f''</span> terms have opposite signs and cancel:\n<span class=\"mb\">D_0 f(x) = f'(x) + \\frac{h^2}{6}f'''(\\xi).</span>\nThe error is now proportional to <span class=\"m\">h^2</span>: a <strong>second-order</strong> method. Halving <span class=\"m\">h</span> cuts the error by a factor of four. For <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{9.61 - 8.41}{0.2} = 6</span> exactly, because <span class=\"m\">f''' = 0</span>. For <span class=\"m\">f(x) = e^x</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> with <span class=\"m\">h = 0.1</span>, the forward difference gives <span class=\"m\">1.0517</span> (error <span class=\"m\">0.052</span>) but the central difference gives <span class=\"m\">1.00167</span> (error <span class=\"m\">0.0017</span>, matching <span class=\"m\">\\frac{h^2}{6} = 0.00167</span>). For the same two function evaluations, the central difference is about 30 times more accurate here.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Order of accuracy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A finite-difference formula has order <span class=\"m\">p</span> if its truncation error is proportional to <span class=\"m\">h^p</span>. Forward differences are first order; central differences are second order.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-second-derivative\" data-h=\"3\">The second derivative</h3>\n<p>Adding the Taylor expansions of <span class=\"m\">f(x + h)</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(x - h)</span> cancels the odd terms instead, giving\n<span class=\"mb\">f''(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x + h) - 2f(x) + f(x - h)}{h^2},</span>\nwith error <span class=\"m\">\\frac{h^2}{12}f''''</span>. This formula is the building block of numerical methods for heat flow, waves and diffusion, and it is how image-processing software detects edges (the discrete Laplacian).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-step-size-trade-off\" data-h=\"3\">The step-size trade-off</h3>\n<p>In exact arithmetic we would just make <span class=\"m\">h</span> tiny. On a computer, <span class=\"m\">f(x + h)</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span> are each stored with a relative rounding error of about <span class=\"m\">u \\approx 10^{-16}</span>. When <span class=\"m\">h</span> is small the two values are nearly equal, and subtracting them is catastrophic cancellation: the rounding error in the numerator is about <span class=\"m\">u|f|</span>, and dividing by <span class=\"m\">h</span> blows it up to about <span class=\"m\">\\frac{u|f|}{h}</span>. The total error of the forward difference is therefore roughly\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac h2|f''| + \\frac{2u|f|}{h}.</span>\nThe first term falls as <span class=\"m\">h</span> shrinks, the second rises. The best compromise is near <span class=\"m\">h \\approx \\sqrt u \\approx 10^{-8}</span>, where the total error is also about <span class=\"m\">10^{-8}</span>: only half of the 16 available digits. For the central difference the balance point is near <span class=\"m\">h \\approx u^{1/3} \\approx 10^{-5}</span>, with error about <span class=\"m\">10^{-10}</span> to <span class=\"m\">10^{-11}</span>. Going smaller makes things worse, which the widget below shows vividly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"richardson-extrapolation\" data-h=\"3\">Richardson extrapolation</h3>\n<p>If we know the error behaves like <span class=\"m\">Ch^2</span>, we can cancel it. Let <span class=\"m\">D(h)</span> be the central difference. Then <span class=\"m\">D(h) \\approx f' + Ch^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">D(h/2) \\approx f' + Ch^2/4</span>, so\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{4D(h/2) - D(h)}{3} \\approx f'</span>\nwith the <span class=\"m\">h^2</span> term gone. For <span class=\"m\">e^x</span> at 0: <span class=\"m\">D(0.1) \\approx 1.0016675</span> and <span class=\"m\">D(0.05) \\approx 1.0004167</span>, and the combination gives <span class=\"m\">0.9999998</span>, an error of only <span class=\"m\">2 \\times 10^{-7}</span>. This idea of combining estimates at different step sizes reappears in Romberg integration and in adaptive ODE solvers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"automatic-differentiation\" data-h=\"3\">Automatic differentiation</h3>\n<p>Machine learning rarely uses finite differences for training. A network with a million parameters would need a million extra function evaluations per gradient. Instead, frameworks use <strong>automatic differentiation</strong>: they apply the chain rule to every elementary operation in the program, giving derivatives that are exact up to rounding at the cost of a few function evaluations. Reverse-mode automatic differentiation is <strong>backpropagation</strong>. Finite differences remain essential for one job: <strong>gradient checking</strong>, testing that a hand-written or automatic gradient is correct.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Choosing <span class=\"m\">h = 10^{-15}</span> \"for accuracy\". Below about <span class=\"m\">10^{-8}</span> (forward) or <span class=\"m\">10^{-5}</span> (central) rounding dominates.</li>\n<li>Using a fixed absolute <span class=\"m\">h</span> when <span class=\"m\">x</span> is huge; scale it, for example <span class=\"m\">h = \\sqrt u\\,\\max(1, |x|)</span>.</li>\n<li>Differentiating noisy measured data directly. Noise of size <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> becomes error of size <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/h</span>; smooth or fit first.</li>\n<li>Confusing automatic differentiation with symbolic or numerical differentiation. It is neither: it is exact chain-rule bookkeeping on the program.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Finite differences estimate derivatives from function values. The forward difference has error proportional to <span class=\"m\">h</span>, the central difference to <span class=\"m\">h^2</span>, and the second-difference formula estimates <span class=\"m\">f''</span> with error proportional to <span class=\"m\">h^2</span>. Rounding error grows like <span class=\"m\">u/h</span>, so the step size has a sweet spot (about <span class=\"m\">10^{-8}</span> forward, <span class=\"m\">10^{-5}</span> central). Richardson extrapolation cancels the leading error term, and automatic differentiation is the exact alternative used in deep learning.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g07-approx\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;diff&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Forward (orange) and central (purple) difference errors at x = 1, on log-log axes.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <span class=\"m\">\\sin x</span> selected, put the pink line at <span class=\"m\">h = 10^{-2}</span> and read the forward and central estimates. Compare their errors with <span class=\"m\">\\frac h2|f''|</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{h^2}{6}|f'''|</span>.\n2. Slide from <span class=\"m\">h = 1</span> to <span class=\"m\">h = 10^{-4}</span>. On the right side the orange line falls with slope 1 and the purple with slope 2: first and second order.\n3. Find each curve's minimum: about <span class=\"m\">10^{-8}</span> for forward and about <span class=\"m\">10^{-5}</span> for central.\n4. Switch to <span class=\"m\">x^3</span>. The central difference now has error exactly <span class=\"m\">h^2</span> (since <span class=\"m\">f''' = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{6h^2}{6} = h^2</span>), while the forward error is about <span class=\"m\">3h</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the right half of each V is truncation error and the left half is rounding error. The slopes tell you the order of the method, and the bottom of the V tells you the best achievable accuracy, exactly as predicted by the error formula in the article.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"tangent\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A secant line approaching the tangent: the geometric picture of a forward difference.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start with a large <span class=\"m\">h</span> and read the secant slope; compare it with the tangent slope.\n2. Shrink <span class=\"m\">h</span> step by step and watch the secant swing toward the tangent.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the secant slope is exactly the forward difference. Its distance from the tangent slope shrinks roughly in proportion to <span class=\"m\">h</span>, the first-order error from the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h} = f'(x) + \\frac h2f''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{f(x + h) - f(x - h)}{2h} = f'(x) + \\frac{h^2}{6}f'''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{f(x + h) - 2f(x) + f(x - h)}{h^2} = f''(x) + \\frac{h^2}{12}f''''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h_{\\text{opt}} \\approx \\sqrt{u}\\ \\text{(forward)},\\quad u^{1/3}\\ \\text{(central)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Richardson: } \\frac{4D(h/2) - D(h)}{3}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Error of the central difference</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> has a continuous third derivative near <span class=\"m\">x</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{f(x + h) - f(x - h)}{2h} - f'(x) = \\frac{h^2}{6}f'''(\\xi)</span> for some <span class=\"m\">\\xi \\in (x - h, x + h)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By Taylor's theorem with remainder, <span class=\"m\">f(x \\pm h) = f(x) \\pm hf'(x) + \\frac{h^2}{2}f''(x) \\pm \\frac{h^3}{6}f'''(\\xi_\\pm)</span>. Subtracting, <span class=\"m\">f(x + h) - f(x - h) = 2hf'(x) + \\frac{h^3}{6}\\big(f'''(\\xi_+) + f'''(\\xi_-)\\big)</span>. Divide by <span class=\"m\">2h</span>; by the intermediate value theorem the average <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(f'''(\\xi_+) + f'''(\\xi_-))</span> equals <span class=\"m\">f'''(\\xi)</span> for some <span class=\"m\">\\xi</span> in between, giving the result.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Forward and central differences for eˣ at x = 0 with h = 0.1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">e^{0.1} \\approx 1.105171</span>, <span class=\"m\">e^{-0.1} \\approx 0.904837</span>; the true derivative is 1.</li>\n<li>Forward: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1.105171 - 1}{0.1} = 1.05171</span>, error <span class=\"m\">\\approx 0.052 \\approx \\frac h2</span>.</li>\n<li>Central: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1.105171 - 0.904837}{0.2} = 1.00167</span>, error <span class=\"m\">\\approx 0.00167 \\approx \\frac{h^2}{6}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Effect of halving h</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Forward differences are first order: error <span class=\"m\">\\approx Ch</span>, so halving <span class=\"m\">h</span> halves the error.</li>\n<li>Central differences are second order: error <span class=\"m\">\\approx Ch^2</span>, so halving <span class=\"m\">h</span> divides the error by 4.</li>\n<li>Check with <span class=\"m\">e^x</span> at 0: central error at <span class=\"m\">h = 0.1</span> is <span class=\"m\">0.001668</span>, at <span class=\"m\">h = 0.05</span> it is <span class=\"m\">0.000417</span>, a ratio of 4.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Estimate <span class=\"m\">f''(1)</span> for <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^4</span> using the second-difference formula with <span class=\"m\">h = 0.1</span>. Compare with the exact value and with the error formula <span class=\"m\">\\frac{h^2}{12}f''''</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">f(1.1) = 1.4641</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(1) = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(0.9) = 0.6561</span>. Estimate <span class=\"m\">= \\frac{1.4641 - 2 + 0.6561}{0.01} = \\frac{0.1202}{0.01} = 12.02</span>. Exact: <span class=\"m\">f''(x) = 12x^2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f''(1) = 12</span>, error <span class=\"m\">0.02</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">f'''' = 24</span> everywhere, the formula predicts <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.01}{12}\\cdot 24 = 0.02</span>, matching exactly.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Gradient checking:</strong> compare each backprop gradient component with <span class=\"m\">\\frac{L(\\theta + he_i) - L(\\theta - he_i)}{2h}</span> using <span class=\"m\">h \\approx 10^{-5}</span> in double precision; a relative difference around <span class=\"m\">10^{-7}</span> or below means the gradient is almost certainly right.</li>\n<li><strong>Backpropagation</strong> is reverse-mode automatic differentiation: one backward pass gives the exact gradient with respect to all parameters.</li>\n<li><strong>Zeroth-order optimization</strong> (for black-box models or hyperparameters) estimates gradients from finite differences or random perturbations.</li>\n<li><strong>Hessian-vector products</strong> can be approximated by <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\nabla L(\\theta + hv) - \\nabla L(\\theta - hv)}{2h}</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Finite differences turn differential equations into systems of algebraic equations on a grid: the heat equation, wave equation and Laplace's equation are all discretised with the second-difference formula. In images, the same formula in two dimensions is the Laplacian filter used for edge detection and sharpening.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Your phone estimates walking speed from GPS positions taken about once a second, a forward or central difference of position. Because GPS positions are noisy by a few metres, differencing over very short intervals gives jumpy speeds, so the software smooths over several seconds: the step-size trade-off in daily life.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Forward difference for f(x) = x² at x = 3 with h = 0.1?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6.1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{9.61 - 9}{0.1} = 6.1</span>; the error equals <span class=\"m\">\\frac h2 f'' = 0.1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Central difference for f(x) = x² at x = 3 with h = 0.1?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{9.61 - 8.41}{0.2} = 6</span>, exact because <span class=\"m\">f''' = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the order of accuracy of the central difference?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Its error is proportional to <span class=\"m\">h^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Halving h in a central difference multiplies the truncation error by about:</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/4|0.25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(h/2)^2 = h^2/4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In double precision, roughly which step size minimises the total error of a forward difference?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">10⁻¹⁶</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">10⁻⁸</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">10⁻²</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The balance point is about <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt u \\approx 10^{-8}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How do deep-learning frameworks compute gradients for training?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Forward differences</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Symbolic algebra</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Reverse-mode automatic differentiation (backpropagation)</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is exact up to rounding and costs only a few forward passes.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "quadrature": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"areas-without-antiderivatives\" data-h=\"3\">Areas without antiderivatives</h3>\n<p>The fundamental theorem of calculus says <span class=\"m\">\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)</span> when we know an antiderivative <span class=\"m\">F</span>. But many important integrals have no elementary antiderivative at all: <span class=\"m\">\\int e^{-x^2}dx</span>, the area under the bell curve, is the famous example. Others involve functions known only by measurements or by a computer program. <strong>Numerical integration</strong>, also called <strong>quadrature</strong>, estimates the integral from function values at chosen points. It is how calculators compute normal probabilities, how engineers compute volumes and work, and how machine learning computes expected values.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you want the area of an irregular garden pond. You stretch a tape across it and measure its width every metre along its length: 0, 3.0, 4.0, 3.5 and 0 metres. A simple estimate treats each one-metre strip as a rectangle. A better one treats each strip as a trapezoid, with the two measured widths as its parallel sides: <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot\\left(\\frac{0}{2} + 3.0 + 4.0 + 3.5 + \\frac02\\right) = 10.5</span> square metres. An even better one fits a parabola through each pair of strips. Every method in this lesson is a version of this idea: replace the curve by simple shapes whose areas we know.</p>\n<h3 id=\"riemann-sums-and-the-midpoint-rule\" data-h=\"3\">Riemann sums and the midpoint rule</h3>\n<p>Split <span class=\"m\">[a, b]</span> into <span class=\"m\">n</span> strips of width <span class=\"m\">h = \\frac{b - a}{n}</span>. The <strong>left rule</strong> uses the height at the left edge of each strip, the <strong>right rule</strong> at the right edge, and the <strong>midpoint rule</strong> at the middle:\n<span class=\"mb\">M_n = h\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f\\big(a + (i - \\tfrac12)h\\big).</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1 x^2\\,dx = \\frac13</span> with <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span> (<span class=\"m\">h = 0.5</span>): the left rule gives <span class=\"m\">0.5(0 + 0.25) = 0.125</span>, very poor; the midpoint rule gives <span class=\"m\">0.5(0.0625 + 0.5625) = 0.3125</span>, an error of only <span class=\"m\">0.021</span>. Left and right rules are first order (error proportional to <span class=\"m\">h</span>); the midpoint rule is second order, because on each strip it overestimates on one side and underestimates on the other, and the first-order errors cancel.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-trapezoid-rule\" data-h=\"3\">The trapezoid rule</h3>\n<p>Join neighbouring points by straight lines and add the trapezoid areas:\n<span class=\"mb\">T_n = h\\left[\\frac{f(x_0)}{2} + f(x_1) + \\cdots + f(x_{n-1}) + \\frac{f(x_n)}{2}\\right].</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1 x^2\\,dx</span> with <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">T_2 = 0.5\\left(0 + 0.25 + 0.5\\right) = 0.375</span>, an error of <span class=\"m\">0.0417</span>. The error formula is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_a^b f\\,dx - T_n = -\\frac{(b - a)h^2}{12}f''(\\xi),</span>\nwhich here gives <span class=\"m\">-\\frac{1\\cdot0.25}{12}\\cdot2 = -0.0417</span>: exact, since <span class=\"m\">f''</span> is constant. The trapezoid rule is also second order, with an error about twice the midpoint rule's and of opposite sign. Halving <span class=\"m\">h</span> divides both errors by about 4.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Quadrature rule</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A formula <span class=\"m\">\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx \\approx \\sum_i w_i f(x_i)</span> that estimates an integral from function values at nodes <span class=\"m\">x_i</span> with weights <span class=\"m\">w_i</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"simpson-s-rule\" data-h=\"3\">Simpson's rule</h3>\n<p>Since the midpoint error is about <span class=\"m\">-\\frac12</span> of the trapezoid error, the combination <span class=\"m\">\\frac{2M + T}{3}</span> cancels the <span class=\"m\">h^2</span> terms. The result is <strong>Simpson's rule</strong>, which fits a parabola through each group of three points. With an even number <span class=\"m\">n</span> of strips,\n<span class=\"mb\">S_n = \\frac h3\\left[f(x_0) + 4f(x_1) + 2f(x_2) + 4f(x_3) + \\cdots + 4f(x_{n-1}) + f(x_n)\\right].</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1 x^2\\,dx</span> with <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.5}{3}(0 + 4\\cdot0.25 + 1) = \\frac13</span>, exact. Simpson's rule is exact for every cubic and has error <span class=\"m\">-\\frac{(b - a)h^4}{180}f''''(\\xi)</span>: <strong>fourth order</strong>. Halving <span class=\"m\">h</span> divides the error by 16. For <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^\\pi\\sin x\\,dx = 2</span>, Simpson with <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span> already gives <span class=\"m\">2.0046</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Simpson's rule</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The quadrature rule <span class=\"m\">\\frac h3[f_0 + 4f_1 + 2f_2 + 4f_3 + \\cdots + f_n]</span> (with <span class=\"m\">n</span> even), which integrates a parabola through each pair of strips exactly and has error proportional to <span class=\"m\">h^4</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"gaussian-quadrature\" data-h=\"3\">Gaussian quadrature</h3>\n<p>So far the nodes were evenly spaced. If we may choose the nodes too, we can do much better. <strong>Gauss–Legendre quadrature</strong> with <span class=\"m\">n</span> well-chosen nodes integrates every polynomial of degree up to <span class=\"m\">2n - 1</span> exactly. With just two nodes at <span class=\"m\">\\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}</span> and weights 1, <span class=\"m\">\\int_{-1}^1 f\\,dx \\approx f(-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}) + f(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3})</span> is exact for cubics: for <span class=\"m\">x^2</span> it gives <span class=\"m\">\\frac13 + \\frac13 = \\frac23</span>, the true value. Gaussian rules are the method of choice for smooth integrands, and <strong>Gauss–Hermite</strong> quadrature handles integrals against a Gaussian weight <span class=\"m\">e^{-x^2}</span>, which appear constantly in statistics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"adaptivity-and-the-curse-of-dimensionality\" data-h=\"3\">Adaptivity and the curse of dimensionality</h3>\n<p>Real software uses <strong>adaptive quadrature</strong>: estimate each piece twice (say, with Simpson on <span class=\"m\">h</span> and <span class=\"m\">h/2</span>), and subdivide only where the two disagree. Effort goes where the function is difficult. In many dimensions, though, grid-based rules break down. A grid with just 10 points per axis needs <span class=\"m\">10^{d}</span> function evaluations in <span class=\"m\">d</span> dimensions: <span class=\"m\">10^{10}</span> for <span class=\"m\">d = 10</span>, and hopelessly many for the millions of dimensions of a neural network's parameter space. This <strong>curse of dimensionality</strong> is why high-dimensional integrals are done by Monte Carlo, the final lesson of this course.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Using Simpson's rule with an odd number of strips. It needs pairs.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the halves on the end values of the trapezoid rule.</li>\n<li>Expecting high-order rules to help with a kink or a jump. Error formulas assume smooth derivatives; split the interval at the trouble spot.</li>\n<li>Using grids for integrals in more than a handful of dimensions.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Quadrature replaces <span class=\"m\">\\int f</span> with a weighted sum of function values. Left and right rules are first order; midpoint and trapezoid are second order with errors of opposite sign; Simpson's rule combines them to fourth order and is exact for cubics. Gaussian quadrature chooses the nodes to be exact up to degree <span class=\"m\">2n - 1</span>, adaptive methods concentrate effort where needed, and in high dimensions Monte Carlo takes over.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"riemann\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Switch rules and change n; the readout compares the estimate with the exact integral.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span> with upper limit <span class=\"m\">b = 2</span> (exact area <span class=\"m\">\\frac83 \\approx 2.667</span>). With 4 rectangles compare Left, Right, Midpoint and Trapezoid.\n2. Check the trapezoid value: <span class=\"m\">0.5\\left(0 + 0.25 + 1 + 2.25 + \\frac42\\right) = 2.75</span>, an error of <span class=\"m\">0.083</span>, as the formula <span class=\"m\">\\frac{(b - a)h^2}{12}f''</span> predicts.\n3. Double n from 4 to 8 and then 16. Left and right errors halve; midpoint and trapezoid errors drop by about 4.\n4. Notice that midpoint and trapezoid errors have opposite signs, with the trapezoid error about twice as big.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> steps 3 and 4 are exactly the facts used to build Simpson's rule. Averaging <span class=\"m\">\\frac{2M + T}{3}</span> cancels the leading error, which is why Simpson's is fourth order.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">M_n = h\\sum_{i=1}^n f(a + (i - \\tfrac12)h),\\quad E_M = \\frac{(b - a)h^2}{24}f''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">T_n = h\\left[\\tfrac12f_0 + f_1 + \\cdots + f_{n-1} + \\tfrac12f_n\\right],\\quad E_T = -\\frac{(b - a)h^2}{12}f''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S_n = \\frac h3[f_0 + 4f_1 + 2f_2 + \\cdots + 4f_{n-1} + f_n],\\quad E_S = -\\frac{(b - a)h^4}{180}f''''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S = \\frac{2M + T}{3}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_{-1}^1 f\\,dx \\approx f\\big(-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\big) + f\\big(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\big)\\ \\ \\text{(exact to degree 3)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Simpson's rule is exact for cubics</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For every polynomial <span class=\"m\">f</span> of degree at most 3, <span class=\"m\">\\int_{-h}^{h} f\\,dx = \\frac h3\\big[f(-h) + 4f(0) + f(h)\\big]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Both sides are linear in <span class=\"m\">f</span>, so it suffices to check <span class=\"m\">1, x, x^2, x^3</span>. For <span class=\"m\">1</span>: <span class=\"m\">2h = \\frac h3(6)</span>. For <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">x^3</span>: both sides are 0 by symmetry. For <span class=\"m\">x^2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\int_{-h}^h x^2dx = \\frac{2h^3}{3}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac h3(h^2 + 0 + h^2) = \\frac{2h^3}{3}</span>. Shifting the interval doesn't change this, so the composite rule is exact for cubics on every pair of strips.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">∫₀^π sin x dx with the trapezoid and Simpson rules</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Exact value: <span class=\"m\">[-\\cos x]_0^\\pi = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Trapezoid, <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">h = \\frac\\pi2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac\\pi2\\left(0 + 1 + 0\\right) \\approx 1.571</span>, error <span class=\"m\">0.43</span>.</li>\n<li>Simpson, <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\pi}{6}(0 + 4\\cdot1 + 0) \\approx 2.094</span>, error <span class=\"m\">0.094</span>.</li>\n<li>Simpson, <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">h = \\frac\\pi4</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\left(0 + 4(0.7071) + 2(1) + 4(0.7071) + 0\\right) \\approx 2.0046</span>, error <span class=\"m\">0.0046</span>, about 20 times smaller than with <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span>, close to the factor 16 that fourth-order convergence predicts for halving <span class=\"m\">h</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Two-point Gaussian quadrature on x⁴</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Exact: <span class=\"m\">\\int_{-1}^1 x^4\\,dx = \\frac25 = 0.4</span>.</li>\n<li>Gauss: <span class=\"m\">2\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)^4 = 2\\cdot\\frac19 = \\frac29 \\approx 0.222</span>.</li>\n<li>Not exact: degree 4 exceeds <span class=\"m\">2n - 1 = 3</span>. For <span class=\"m\">x^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">x^3</span> the same rule is exact.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Estimate <span class=\"m\">\\ln 2 = \\int_1^2\\frac{dx}{x}</span> with (a) the trapezoid rule and (b) Simpson's rule, both with <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span>. Compare with <span class=\"m\">\\ln 2 \\approx 0.693147</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">h = 0.5</span>, values <span class=\"m\">f(1) = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(1.5) = \\frac23</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(2) = 0.5</span>. (a) <span class=\"m\">T_2 = 0.5\\left(\\frac12 + \\frac23 + \\frac14\\right) = 0.5(1.41667) \\approx 0.70833</span>, error <span class=\"m\">\\approx 0.0152</span> (too big, since <span class=\"m\">\\frac1x</span> is convex and chords lie above it). (b) <span class=\"m\">S_2 = \\frac{0.5}{3}\\left(1 + 4\\cdot\\frac23 + 0.5\\right) = \\frac16(4.16667) \\approx 0.69444</span>, error <span class=\"m\">\\approx 0.0013</span>, more than ten times smaller for the same three function values.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>ROC AUC</strong> is computed with the trapezoid rule over the points of the ROC curve (for example <code>sklearn.metrics.auc</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Expectations under Gaussians</strong> in Bayesian models, Gaussian-process classification and variational inference are often computed with Gauss–Hermite quadrature.</li>\n<li><strong>Normalizing constants and marginal likelihoods</strong> in low dimensions are found by quadrature; in high dimensions they need Monte Carlo or variational bounds.</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion and neural ODE models</strong> integrate over time, which is quadrature applied along a trajectory.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Library functions such as SciPy's <code>quad</code> use adaptive Gauss–Kronrod rules: a Gauss rule and an extended rule share nodes, their difference estimates the error, and intervals are bisected until the estimate meets the tolerance.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Simpson's rule has been used in naval architecture for centuries to compute a ship's displacement and stability from cross-section measurements taken at regular stations along the hull, exactly the garden-pond idea at a larger scale.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Trapezoid rule with one strip for ∫₀¹ x² dx?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/2|0.5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1\\cdot\\frac{0 + 1}{2} = \\frac12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Midpoint rule with one strip for ∫₀¹ x² dx?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/4|0.25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1\\cdot(0.5)^2 = \\frac14</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Up to what degree is Simpson's rule exact?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is exact for all cubics, thanks to symmetry.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Halving h in Simpson's rule divides the error by about:</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Fourth order: <span class=\"m\">2^4 = 16</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Halving h in the trapezoid rule divides the error by about:</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Second order: <span class=\"m\">2^2 = 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why is Monte Carlo preferred for integrals in high dimensions?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Grid rules need a number of points that grows exponentially with dimension</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Monte Carlo is exact</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Simpson's rule only works in one dimension by definition</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>With 10 points per axis, <span class=\"m\">d</span> dimensions need <span class=\"m\">10^d</span> points.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "ode-solvers": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"predicting-the-future-one-small-step-at-a-time\" data-h=\"3\">Predicting the future one small step at a time</h3>\n<p>A <strong>differential equation</strong> describes how something changes: the rate of change of a population depends on its size, the acceleration of a planet depends on its position, the rate of change of a neural network's weights depends on the gradient of the loss. Written as <span class=\"m\">y'(t) = f(t, y)</span> with a known starting value <span class=\"m\">y(0) = y_0</span>, it is called an <strong>initial value problem</strong>. Only a few such equations have formula solutions. For the rest, including weather models, epidemic models, circuits and orbits, we compute the solution numerically by marching forward in small time steps.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine driving in fog with a map that tells you, at any point, which direction the road goes. You can't see the whole road, but you can look at the map, drive straight in the indicated direction for 10 metres, check the map again, and repeat. If the road curves gently and your steps are short, you'll follow it closely. If your steps are long, you'll overshoot bends and drift off the road. That is <strong>Euler's method</strong>: follow the current slope for one step, then re-evaluate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"euler-s-method\" data-h=\"3\">Euler's method</h3>\n<p>Starting from <span class=\"m\">(t_0, y_0)</span> with step size <span class=\"m\">h</span>, Euler's method uses the tangent line:\n<span class=\"mb\">y_{n+1} = y_n + h f(t_n, y_n),\\qquad t_{n+1} = t_n + h.</span>\nLet's solve <span class=\"m\">y' = y</span>, <span class=\"m\">y(0) = 1</span>, whose true solution is <span class=\"m\">y = e^t</span>, up to <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span>. With <span class=\"m\">h = 0.5</span>: <span class=\"m\">y_1 = 1 + 0.5(1) = 1.5</span>, then <span class=\"m\">y_2 = 1.5 + 0.5(1.5) = 2.25</span>. The true value is <span class=\"m\">e \\approx 2.718</span>, so the error is <span class=\"m\">0.468</span>. With <span class=\"m\">h = 0.25</span> we get <span class=\"m\">1.25^4 \\approx 2.441</span> (error <span class=\"m\">0.277</span>); with <span class=\"m\">h = 0.1</span>, <span class=\"m\">1.1^{10} \\approx 2.594</span> (error <span class=\"m\">0.125</span>). Each time the step shrinks, the error shrinks roughly in proportion. Euler's method is <strong>first order</strong>: its global error is proportional to <span class=\"m\">h</span>.</p>\n<p>Where does the error come from? Each step follows a straight line while the true curve bends, giving a <strong>local error</strong> of about <span class=\"m\">\\frac{h^2}{2}y''</span> per step. Over <span class=\"m\">\\frac{T}{h}</span> steps these add up to a <strong>global error</strong> proportional to <span class=\"m\">h</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Euler's method</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The time-stepping rule <span class=\"m\">y_{n+1} = y_n + hf(t_n, y_n)</span> for <span class=\"m\">y' = f(t, y)</span>: follow the slope at the current point for one step. Its global error is proportional to <span class=\"m\">h</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"runge-kutta-methods\" data-h=\"3\">Runge–Kutta methods</h3>\n<p>To do better, sample the slope at several points within the step and average them cleverly. <strong>Heun's method</strong> (improved Euler) takes a trial Euler step, evaluates the slope at its end, and averages the two slopes:\n<span class=\"mb\">k_1 = f(t_n, y_n),\\quad k_2 = f(t_n + h, y_n + hk_1),\\quad y_{n+1} = y_n + \\frac h2(k_1 + k_2).</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">y' = y</span> with <span class=\"m\">h = 0.5</span>, each step multiplies by <span class=\"m\">1 + h + \\frac{h^2}{2} = 1.625</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">1.625^2 \\approx 2.641</span> at <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span>, an error of <span class=\"m\">0.078</span>: six times better than Euler with the same step. Heun's method is second order.</p>\n<p>The workhorse is the classical <strong>fourth-order Runge–Kutta method</strong> (RK4), which uses four slope samples per step:\n<span class=\"mb\">k_1 = f(t_n, y_n),\\ k_2 = f(t_n + \\tfrac h2, y_n + \\tfrac h2k_1),\\ k_3 = f(t_n + \\tfrac h2, y_n + \\tfrac h2k_2),\\ k_4 = f(t_n + h, y_n + hk_3),</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">y_{n+1} = y_n + \\frac h6(k_1 + 2k_2 + 2k_3 + k_4).</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">y' = y</span>, one RK4 step multiplies by <span class=\"m\">1 + h + \\frac{h^2}{2} + \\frac{h^3}{6} + \\frac{h^4}{24}</span>, the Taylor series of <span class=\"m\">e^h</span> up to <span class=\"m\">h^4</span>. With <span class=\"m\">h = 0.5</span> that is <span class=\"m\">1.6484</span>, and two steps give <span class=\"m\">2.7173</span>, an error of only <span class=\"m\">0.0009</span>. Halving the step divides RK4's error by about 16. Modern solvers such as <code>RK45</code> pair two Runge–Kutta formulas of different orders to estimate the error and choose the step size automatically.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Runge–Kutta method</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A one-step ODE method that combines several slope evaluations within each step to match the Taylor series of the true solution to higher order. RK4 is fourth order.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"stability-and-stiffness\" data-h=\"3\">Stability and stiffness</h3>\n<p>Accuracy is not the only concern. Consider the decaying equation <span class=\"m\">y' = -\\lambda y</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda &gt; 0</span>, whose solution <span class=\"m\">y_0e^{-\\lambda t}</span> shrinks to zero. Euler gives <span class=\"m\">y_{n+1} = (1 - h\\lambda)y_n</span>. If <span class=\"m\">h\\lambda &lt; 2</span>, then <span class=\"m\">|1 - h\\lambda| &lt; 1</span> and the numerical solution decays too. But if <span class=\"m\">h &gt; \\frac{2}{\\lambda}</span>, the factor is less than <span class=\"m\">-1</span> and the numbers oscillate and <strong>grow without bound</strong>, even though the true solution decays. With <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 10</span> and <span class=\"m\">h = 0.3</span>, the factor is <span class=\"m\">-2</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> goes <span class=\"m\">1, -2, 4, -8, \\ldots</span>. This is <strong>numerical instability</strong>.</p>\n<p>An equation is <strong>stiff</strong> when it contains both very fast and slow processes, such as chemical reactions with very different rates. The fast part forces explicit methods like Euler to take tiny steps for stability, long after it has died away. <strong>Implicit methods</strong> fix this. <strong>Backward Euler</strong> evaluates the slope at the <em>new</em> point, <span class=\"m\">y_{n+1} = y_n + hf(t_{n+1}, y_{n+1})</span>, which for <span class=\"m\">y' = -\\lambda y</span> gives <span class=\"m\">y_{n+1} = \\frac{y_n}{1 + h\\lambda}</span>: stable for every step size. The price is solving an equation (often with Newton's method) at every step.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Stiff equation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A differential equation whose solution has components changing on very different time scales, so that explicit methods need tiny steps for stability rather than accuracy.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"gradient-descent-is-euler-s-method\" data-h=\"3\">Gradient descent is Euler's method</h3>\n<p>Here is a connection that matters for machine learning. The <strong>gradient flow</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\theta'(t) = -\\nabla L(\\theta)</span> describes a ball rolling downhill on the loss surface. Apply Euler's method with step <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\theta_{n+1} = \\theta_n - \\eta\\nabla L(\\theta_n)</span>. That is exactly <strong>gradient descent</strong>, with the learning rate as the step size. The stability analysis carries over: on a quadratic loss <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\theta^2</span>, gradient descent multiplies <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> by <span class=\"m\">1 - \\eta\\lambda</span> each step, so it diverges when <span class=\"m\">\\eta &gt; \\frac{2}{\\lambda}</span>. A too-large learning rate is a too-large Euler step, and the curvature <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> plays the role of stiffness.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Assuming small errors per step mean small total error. Local errors accumulate, and instability can amplify them.</li>\n<li>Using an explicit method on a stiff problem and blaming the model when the numbers explode.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that halving the step doubles the work; a higher-order method is usually a better investment.</li>\n<li>Using plain Euler for long-time physics such as orbits. It steadily gains energy; symplectic methods like leapfrog are designed to avoid that.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Initial value problems <span class=\"m\">y' = f(t, y)</span> are solved by stepping forward in time. Euler's method follows the current slope and is first order; Heun's method is second order and RK4 fourth order. Explicit methods are unstable when the step is too large relative to the fastest decay rate (<span class=\"m\">h &gt; 2/\\lambda</span> for Euler); implicit methods such as backward Euler stay stable on stiff problems. Gradient descent is Euler's method on gradient flow, and its learning-rate limit is this stability limit.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Gradient descent as Euler steps on the gradient flow of f = x² + 2y².</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run with learning rate <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.1</span> and watch smooth Euler steps roll into the minimum.\n2. The steepest direction has curvature <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 4</span> (since <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial y^2} = 4</span>), so the stability limit is <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; \\frac{2}{4} = 0.5</span>. Try <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.4</span>: the path zig-zags across the valley but still converges.\n3. Try <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.55</span>: the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-coordinate is multiplied by <span class=\"m\">1 - 4(0.55) = -1.2</span> each step and the iterates spiral outward.\n4. Switch to the ravine surface <span class=\"m\">0.2x^2 + 4y^2</span>. Its curvatures are <span class=\"m\">0.4</span> and <span class=\"m\">8</span>, so stability needs <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 0.25</span>, but then the gentle direction crawls. That is stiffness.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the divergence in step 3 is exactly the Euler instability <span class=\"m\">|1 - h\\lambda| &gt; 1</span> from the article, with learning rate as the step size. Ill-conditioned problems are stiff problems.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Euler: } y_{n+1} = y_n + hf(t_n, y_n)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Heun: } y_{n+1} = y_n + \\tfrac h2\\big[f(t_n, y_n) + f(t_n + h, y_n + hf(t_n, y_n))\\big]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{RK4: } y_{n+1} = y_n + \\tfrac h6(k_1 + 2k_2 + 2k_3 + k_4)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Backward Euler: } y_{n+1} = y_n + hf(t_{n+1}, y_{n+1})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">y' = -\\lambda y:\\ \\text{Euler stable iff } 0 &lt; h\\lambda \\le 2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Global error: } O(h)\\ \\text{Euler},\\ O(h^2)\\ \\text{Heun},\\ O(h^4)\\ \\text{RK4}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Stability of Euler's method on the test equation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">y' = -\\lambda y</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda &gt; 0</span>, Euler's method gives <span class=\"m\">y_n = (1 - h\\lambda)^ny_0</span>, which tends to 0 if <span class=\"m\">0 &lt; h &lt; \\frac{2}{\\lambda}</span> and grows without bound if <span class=\"m\">h &gt; \\frac{2}{\\lambda}</span>. Backward Euler gives <span class=\"m\">y_n = (1 + h\\lambda)^{-n}y_0</span>, which tends to 0 for every <span class=\"m\">h &gt; 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Euler: <span class=\"m\">y_{n+1} = y_n + h(-\\lambda y_n) = (1 - h\\lambda)y_n</span>, so <span class=\"m\">y_n = (1 - h\\lambda)^ny_0</span>. This tends to 0 iff <span class=\"m\">|1 - h\\lambda| &lt; 1</span>, i.e. <span class=\"m\">0 &lt; h\\lambda &lt; 2</span>, and grows iff <span class=\"m\">h\\lambda &gt; 2</span>. Backward Euler: <span class=\"m\">y_{n+1} = y_n - h\\lambda y_{n+1}</span> gives <span class=\"m\">y_{n+1} = \\frac{y_n}{1 + h\\lambda}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">0 &lt; \\frac{1}{1 + h\\lambda} &lt; 1</span> for all <span class=\"m\">h &gt; 0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Euler's method for y′ = y, y(0) = 1 to t = 1 with h = 0.5 and h = 0.25</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">h = 0.5</span>: each step multiplies by <span class=\"m\">1.5</span>: <span class=\"m\">y(1) \\approx 1.5^2 = 2.25</span>, error <span class=\"m\">0.468</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">h = 0.25</span>: each step multiplies by <span class=\"m\">1.25</span>: <span class=\"m\">y(1) \\approx 1.25^4 \\approx 2.441</span>, error <span class=\"m\">0.277</span>.</li>\n<li>The error ratio is about <span class=\"m\">0.6</span>, approaching <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span> as <span class=\"m\">h</span> shrinks: first-order convergence.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">One RK4 step for y′ = y, y(0) = 1, h = 1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">k_1 = 1</span>; <span class=\"m\">k_2 = 1 + \\frac12(1) = 1.5</span>; <span class=\"m\">k_3 = 1 + \\frac12(1.5) = 1.25</span>; <span class=\"m\">k_4 = 1 + 1.25 = 2.25</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">y_1 = 1 + \\frac16(1 + 3 + 2.5 + 2.25) = 1 + \\frac{8.75}{6} \\approx 2.4583</span>.</li>\n<li>True value <span class=\"m\">e \\approx 2.7183</span>. Even with one huge step RK4 beats Euler with ten steps (<span class=\"m\">2.594</span>).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">y' = -2y</span>, <span class=\"m\">y(0) = 1</span>: (a) apply forward Euler with <span class=\"m\">h = 0.25</span> to reach <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span>; (b) apply backward Euler with the same step; (c) compare with <span class=\"m\">e^{-2}</span>; (d) find the largest step for which forward Euler does not blow up.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Factor <span class=\"m\">1 - 2(0.25) = 0.5</span> per step, so <span class=\"m\">y(1) \\approx 0.5^4 = 0.0625</span>. (b) Factor <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{1 + 0.5} = \\frac23</span>, so <span class=\"m\">y(1) \\approx \\left(\\frac23\\right)^4 = \\frac{16}{81} \\approx 0.198</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">e^{-2} \\approx 0.135</span>: forward Euler undershoots and backward Euler overshoots, both by first-order errors. (d) Stability needs <span class=\"m\">h\\lambda \\le 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">h \\le 1</span>; for <span class=\"m\">h &gt; 1</span> the iterates oscillate and grow.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Gradient descent</strong> is Euler's method on gradient flow; momentum methods discretise a heavy-ball ODE, and the learning-rate limit <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 2/\\lambda_{\\max}</span> is Euler's stability condition with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_{\\max}</span> the largest Hessian eigenvalue.</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion models</strong> generate images by integrating a probability-flow ODE backwards in time; sampler names like \"Euler\", \"Heun\" and \"DPM-Solver\" are exactly these ODE methods.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural ODEs</strong> define a network layer as the solution of <span class=\"m\">z' = f_\\theta(z, t)</span> and call an adaptive Runge–Kutta solver in the forward pass.</li>\n<li><strong>Hamiltonian Monte Carlo</strong> uses the leapfrog integrator to simulate a particle moving through parameter space.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Game physics engines update positions and velocities every frame with semi-implicit (symplectic) Euler or Verlet integration, which are cheap and keep energy roughly constant. Too large a time step and objects tunnel through walls or explode, a visible version of numerical instability.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Epidemic forecasts use the SIR model, <span class=\"m\">S' = -\\beta SI</span>, <span class=\"m\">I' = \\beta SI - \\gamma I</span>, <span class=\"m\">R' = \\gamma I</span>, solved numerically by Runge–Kutta methods. Weather forecasts step huge systems of differential equations forward in time steps of minutes, on supercomputers, to predict the next week.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">One Euler step for y′ = y from y(0) = 1 with h = 0.1?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 + 0.1(1) = 1.1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Euler's method for y′ = y, y(0) = 1, h = 0.5, two steps. What is y(1)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2.25|9/4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1.5^2 = 2.25</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the order of the classical RK4 method?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Its global error is proportional to <span class=\"m\">h^4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Forward Euler on y′ = −10y is stable for h up to:</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.2|1/5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Needs <span class=\"m\">h\\lambda \\le 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">h \\le 0.2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why use backward Euler on stiff problems?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It is more accurate per step</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">It is stable for any step size on decaying problems</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It needs no equation solving</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Its factor <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{1 + h\\lambda}</span> is always between 0 and 1.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Gradient descent with learning rate η is:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Euler's method on the gradient flow θ′ = −∇L</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">RK4 on the loss</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Backward Euler on the gradient</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\theta_{n+1} = \\theta_n - \\eta\\nabla L(\\theta_n)</span> is one Euler step of size <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "num-linalg": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-most-common-computation-in-science\" data-h=\"3\">The most common computation in science</h3>\n<p>Solving a system of linear equations <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> is probably the single most frequent task in scientific computing. Fitting a regression model, simulating a bridge, rendering a scene, solving a differential equation and taking a Newton step all end up as linear systems, sometimes with millions of unknowns. You learned to solve small systems by elimination in algebra. This lesson is about doing it on a computer: efficiently, in a way that rounding errors cannot wreck, and with an honest idea of how accurate the answer can be.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine two friends who buy snacks. One buys 2 coffees and 1 muffin for 3 dollars; the other buys 4 coffees and 5 muffins for 9 dollars. What does each item cost? Subtract twice the first purchase from the second: <span class=\"m\">4 - 2\\cdot2 = 0</span> coffees and <span class=\"m\">5 - 2\\cdot1 = 3</span> muffins cost <span class=\"m\">9 - 2\\cdot3 = 3</span> dollars, so a muffin costs 1 dollar, and then <span class=\"m\">2c + 1 = 3</span> gives a coffee at 1 dollar. That is Gaussian elimination. Now imagine the two receipts were almost identical, say 1 coffee and 1 muffin for 2.00 dollars and 1 coffee and 1.0001 muffins for 2.0001 dollars. A typo of one hundredth of a cent on the second receipt would completely change the answer. Some systems are simply sensitive, and no algorithm can fix that.</p>\n<h3 id=\"gaussian-elimination-is-lu-factorization\" data-h=\"3\">Gaussian elimination is LU factorization</h3>\n<p>Elimination subtracts multiples of rows to create zeros below the diagonal, leaving an upper triangular matrix <span class=\"m\">U</span>. If we record the multipliers in a lower triangular matrix <span class=\"m\">L</span> with ones on the diagonal, we get the factorization\n<span class=\"mb\">A = LU.</span>\nFor the snack system, <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\4 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>: the multiplier for row 2 is <span class=\"m\">\\frac42 = 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">L = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">U = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">LU = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\4 &amp; 2 + 3\\end{bmatrix} = A</span>.</p>\n<p>Solving <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> is now two easy triangular solves. First <strong>forward substitution</strong> for <span class=\"m\">Ly = b</span>: <span class=\"m\">y_1 = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">y_2 = 9 - 2\\cdot3 = 3</span>. Then <strong>back substitution</strong> for <span class=\"m\">Ux = y</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_2 = \\frac33 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_1 = \\frac{3 - 1}{2} = 1</span>. The factorization costs about <span class=\"m\">\\frac23n^3</span> arithmetic operations, while each triangular solve costs only about <span class=\"m\">n^2</span>. So once <span class=\"m\">A</span> is factored you can solve for many right-hand sides cheaply.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">LU factorization</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Writing <span class=\"m\">A = LU</span> (or <span class=\"m\">PA = LU</span> with a row permutation <span class=\"m\">P</span>), where <span class=\"m\">L</span> is lower triangular with unit diagonal and <span class=\"m\">U</span> is upper triangular. It is Gaussian elimination with the multipliers saved.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-you-should-never-compute-the-inverse\" data-h=\"3\">Why you should never compute the inverse</h3>\n<p>It's tempting to write <span class=\"m\">x = A^{-1}b</span> in code. Don't. Computing <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span> costs about three times as much as an LU factorization, and multiplying by it is usually less accurate. Libraries provide <code>solve(A, b)</code> precisely so you never form the inverse. When a formula contains <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}B</span>, read it as \"solve <span class=\"m\">AX = B</span>\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"pivoting\" data-h=\"3\">Pivoting</h3>\n<p>Elimination divides by the diagonal entry, the <strong>pivot</strong>. If a pivot is zero the method fails, and if it is merely tiny the method becomes wildly inaccurate. Consider <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}10^{-20} &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}x = \\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, whose solution is very close to <span class=\"m\">x = (1, 1)</span>. Eliminating with the tiny pivot uses the multiplier <span class=\"m\">10^{20}</span>: the new bottom-right entry is <span class=\"m\">1 - 10^{20}</span>, which rounds to <span class=\"m\">-10^{20}</span>, and the right side <span class=\"m\">2 - 10^{20}</span> also rounds to <span class=\"m\">-10^{20}</span>. Back substitution gives <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1</span> and then <span class=\"m\">x_1 = \\frac{1 - 1}{10^{-20}} = 0</span>. Completely wrong. <strong>Partial pivoting</strong> swaps rows so the largest available entry in the column becomes the pivot. Swapping the two rows here gives multiplier <span class=\"m\">10^{-20}</span>, no catastrophe, and the correct answer <span class=\"m\">x \\approx (1, 1)</span>. With partial pivoting, Gaussian elimination is stable in practice, and it is what every library uses: <span class=\"m\">PA = LU</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"special-structure-cholesky\" data-h=\"3\">Special structure: Cholesky</h3>\n<p>Many matrices in statistics and machine learning are <strong>symmetric positive definite</strong> (SPD), such as covariance matrices and <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>. For them there is the <strong>Cholesky factorization</strong> <span class=\"m\">A = LL^T</span>, which costs half as much as LU and needs no pivoting. For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>: <span class=\"m\">L_{11} = \\sqrt4 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_{21} = \\frac22 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_{22} = \\sqrt{3 - 1^2} = \\sqrt2</span>. Cholesky also gives a fast test for positive definiteness: it succeeds exactly when the matrix is SPD.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-condition-number-of-a-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The condition number of a matrix</h3>\n<p>How sensitive is <span class=\"m\">x</span> to small changes in <span class=\"m\">b</span> or <span class=\"m\">A</span>? The <strong>condition number</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\kappa(A) = \\|A\\|\\,\\|A^{-1}\\|</span> answers that. In the 2-norm it is the ratio of the largest to the smallest singular value; for an SPD matrix, the ratio of the largest to smallest eigenvalue. It satisfies\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\|\\Delta x\\|}{\\|x\\|} \\le \\kappa(A)\\frac{\\|\\Delta b\\|}{\\|b\\|}.</span>\nTake <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 1.0001\\end{bmatrix}</span> from the receipts. With <span class=\"m\">b = (2, 2.0001)</span> the solution is <span class=\"m\">x = (1, 1)</span>. Change <span class=\"m\">b_2</span> by just <span class=\"m\">0.0001</span> to <span class=\"m\">2.0002</span> and the solution becomes <span class=\"m\">x = (0, 2)</span>. Its eigenvalues are about <span class=\"m\">2.00005</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.00005</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\kappa \\approx 4 \\times 10^4</span>. The rule of thumb from the error lesson applies: you lose about <span class=\"m\">\\log_{10}\\kappa</span> digits. A matrix with <span class=\"m\">\\kappa \\approx 10^{16}</span> is numerically singular in double precision.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Matrix condition number</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\kappa(A) = \\|A\\|\\,\\|A^{-1}\\| = \\sigma_{\\max}/\\sigma_{\\min}</span> in the 2-norm. It bounds how much relative errors in the data can be amplified in the solution of <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"least-squares-qr-instead-of-normal-equations\" data-h=\"3\">Least squares: QR instead of normal equations</h3>\n<p>When there are more equations than unknowns, we seek the least-squares solution minimizing <span class=\"m\">\\|Ax - b\\|^2</span>. The textbook route is the <strong>normal equations</strong> <span class=\"m\">A^TAx = A^Tb</span>. But <span class=\"m\">\\kappa(A^TA) = \\kappa(A)^2</span>: if <span class=\"m\">\\kappa(A) = 10^6</span>, the normal equations have <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = 10^{12}</span> and lose 12 digits instead of 6. Stable software factors <span class=\"m\">A = QR</span> with orthonormal <span class=\"m\">Q</span> and triangular <span class=\"m\">R</span>, then solves <span class=\"m\">Rx = Q^Tb</span>, which works with <span class=\"m\">\\kappa(A)</span> directly. For extremely ill-conditioned problems the SVD is the safest tool.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Computing <code>inv(A) @ b</code>. Use a solver.</li>\n<li>Eliminating without pivoting. Tiny pivots cause disaster even when the matrix is well-conditioned.</li>\n<li>Confusing a small determinant with ill-conditioning. <span class=\"m\">\\det(0.1I_{10}) = 10^{-10}</span>, but <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = 1</span>; scaling changes the determinant, not the condition number.</li>\n<li>Solving least squares with the normal equations when <span class=\"m\">A</span> is ill-conditioned.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Gaussian elimination factors <span class=\"m\">A = LU</span> in about <span class=\"m\">\\frac23n^3</span> operations, after which each solve is two cheap triangular substitutions. Partial pivoting (<span class=\"m\">PA = LU</span>) makes it stable; Cholesky (<span class=\"m\">A = LL^T</span>) is twice as fast for symmetric positive definite matrices. The condition number <span class=\"m\">\\kappa(A)</span> limits accuracy (about <span class=\"m\">\\log_{10}\\kappa</span> digits lost), and least squares should be solved with QR or the SVD rather than normal equations, which square <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;singular&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the columns of A; as the determinant approaches 0 the plane is squashed toward a line.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start from the singular preset: both columns lie on one line, the unit square has collapsed and <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> has no unique solution.\n2. Drag <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> slightly off the line. The matrix becomes invertible but nearly singular, like the receipts matrix, and the square is a thin sliver.\n3. Rotate <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> until it is perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span>. The square becomes a healthy parallelogram: well-conditioned.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> in the thin-sliver state, many different inputs <span class=\"m\">x</span> land almost on top of each other, so recovering <span class=\"m\">x</span> from <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> is sensitive to tiny errors. That is ill-conditioning. Its size is measured by the stretch ratio <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_{\\max}/\\sigma_{\\min}</span>, not by the determinant alone.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"lstsq\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Least squares: the line minimizing total squared error.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Move the slope and intercept sliders to make the pink squares as small as you can, then compare with the \"best possible\" value.\n2. Drag one point far away and watch the best line tilt toward it.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the best line is the solution of an overdetermined linear system. Software finds it with a QR factorization rather than by forming <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>, for the conditioning reasons in the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">PA = LU,\\quad Ly = Pb,\\quad Ux = y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{cost: } \\tfrac23n^3\\ \\text{(LU)},\\ \\tfrac13n^3\\ \\text{(Cholesky)},\\ n^2\\ \\text{per triangular solve}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = LL^T\\ \\text{for SPD } A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\kappa(A) = \\|A\\|\\|A^{-1}\\| = \\frac{\\sigma_{\\max}}{\\sigma_{\\min}},\\qquad \\frac{\\|\\Delta x\\|}{\\|x\\|} \\le \\kappa(A)\\frac{\\|\\Delta b\\|}{\\|b\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\kappa(A^TA) = \\kappa(A)^2,\\qquad A = QR \\Rightarrow Rx = Q^Tb</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Perturbation bound for linear systems</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> and <span class=\"m\">A(x + \\Delta x) = b + \\Delta b</span> with <span class=\"m\">A</span> invertible and <span class=\"m\">b \\ne 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\|\\Delta x\\|}{\\|x\\|} \\le \\kappa(A)\\frac{\\|\\Delta b\\|}{\\|b\\|}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Subtracting the equations gives <span class=\"m\">A\\Delta x = \\Delta b</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\Delta x = A^{-1}\\Delta b</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|\\Delta x\\| \\le \\|A^{-1}\\|\\|\\Delta b\\|</span>. Also <span class=\"m\">\\|b\\| = \\|Ax\\| \\le \\|A\\|\\|x\\|</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\|x\\|} \\le \\frac{\\|A\\|}{\\|b\\|}</span>. Multiplying the two inequalities gives <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\|\\Delta x\\|}{\\|x\\|} \\le \\|A\\|\\|A^{-1}\\|\\frac{\\|\\Delta b\\|}{\\|b\\|}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Solve [[2, 1], [4, 5]] x = (3, 9) with LU</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Multiplier <span class=\"m\">\\ell_{21} = 4/2 = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">L = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">U = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>Forward: <span class=\"m\">y_1 = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">y_2 = 9 - 2(3) = 3</span>.</li>\n<li>Back: <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 3/3 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_1 = (3 - 1)/2 = 1</span>. So <span class=\"m\">x = (1, 1)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cholesky factor of [[4, 2], [2, 3]]</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L_{11} = \\sqrt{4} = 2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L_{21} = 2/L_{11} = 1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L_{22} = \\sqrt{3 - L_{21}^2} = \\sqrt2</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">LL^T = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1 + 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Least squares line through (0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 2)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = (1, 2, 2)</span>; <span class=\"m\">A^TA = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 3\\\\3 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">A^Tb = (5, 6)</span>.</li>\n<li>Solve: <span class=\"m\">\\det = 6</span>, so <span class=\"m\">(c, m) = \\frac16(5\\cdot5 - 3\\cdot6,\\ -3\\cdot5 + 3\\cdot6) = \\left(\\frac76, \\frac12\\right)</span>.</li>\n<li>Best line <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac76 + \\frac12x</span>. (For a tiny well-conditioned problem the normal equations are fine; QR gives the same answer.)</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Solve <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\3 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}x = \\begin{bmatrix}5\\\\11\\end{bmatrix}</span> by Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting. Give <span class=\"m\">P</span>, <span class=\"m\">L</span>, <span class=\"m\">U</span> and <span class=\"m\">x</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>The largest entry in column 1 is 3, so swap rows: <span class=\"m\">P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 4\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">Pb = (11, 5)</span>. Multiplier <span class=\"m\">\\frac13</span>: <span class=\"m\">L = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\\\frac13 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">U = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 4\\\\0 &amp; 2 - \\frac43\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 4\\\\0 &amp; \\frac23\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Forward: <span class=\"m\">y = (11, 5 - \\frac{11}{3}) = (11, \\frac43)</span>. Back: <span class=\"m\">x_2 = \\frac{4/3}{2/3} = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_1 = \\frac{11 - 8}{3} = 1</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">1 + 4 = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">3 + 8 = 11</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Linear regression</strong> is least squares: libraries solve it with QR or the SVD (<code>numpy.linalg.lstsq</code>), and <strong>ridge regression</strong> adds <span class=\"m\">\\lambda I</span> to <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>, which also improves its condition number.</li>\n<li><strong>Gaussian processes</strong> need <span class=\"m\">K^{-1}y</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\log\\det K</span> for a kernel matrix <span class=\"m\">K</span>; both come from one Cholesky factorization, plus a small \"jitter\" on the diagonal to keep it numerically positive definite.</li>\n<li><strong>Sampling from a multivariate Gaussian</strong> uses the Cholesky factor: <span class=\"m\">x = \\mu + Lz</span> with <span class=\"m\">z</span> standard normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Newton and natural-gradient</strong> steps solve linear systems with the Hessian or Fisher matrix.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>LAPACK, the library behind NumPy, MATLAB and R, implements LU with partial pivoting, Cholesky and QR in blocked form so that most of the work is matrix-matrix multiplication, which runs near peak speed on modern CPUs and GPUs. The LINPACK benchmark used to rank the world's supercomputers is exactly a large LU solve.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Structural engineers model a bridge as thousands of connected beams; the forces and displacements solve a large SPD linear system, typically by Cholesky. GPS receivers compute their position each second by solving a small least-squares system built from the distances to four or more satellites.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Solve 2x + y = 3, 4x + 5y = 9. What is y?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Elimination leaves <span class=\"m\">3y = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Roughly how does the cost of LU factorization grow with n?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">n</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">n²</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">n³</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>About <span class=\"m\">\\frac23n^3</span> operations.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Condition number (2-norm) of diag(10, 0.1)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"100\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sigma_{\\max}/\\sigma_{\\min} = 10/0.1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If κ(A) = 10⁴, what is κ(AᵀA)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"100000000|1e8\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Normal equations square the condition number.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why do solvers use partial pivoting?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">To avoid dividing by tiny pivots that amplify rounding errors</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">To make the matrix symmetric</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">To compute the inverse</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Large multipliers from small pivots destroy accuracy.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Cholesky factorization A = LLᵀ requires A to be:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Diagonal</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Symmetric positive definite</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Upper triangular</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It succeeds exactly for SPD matrices.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "iterative-methods": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"when-direct-methods-are-too-expensive\" data-h=\"3\">When direct methods are too expensive</h3>\n<p>LU factorization costs about <span class=\"m\">\\frac23n^3</span> operations. For <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span> that is under a billion, a fraction of a second. For a million unknowns, which is routine in simulations, image processing and graph problems, it is about <span class=\"m\">7 \\times 10^{17}</span> operations and terabytes of memory. Yet such matrices are usually <strong>sparse</strong>: each row has only a handful of nonzero entries, because each unknown interacts with only a few neighbours. <strong>Iterative methods</strong> exploit this. They start from a guess and improve it using only matrix-vector products, which cost just a few operations per nonzero. A good iterative method reaches useful accuracy long before a direct method would finish.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a row of houses where everyone wants their thermostat set to the average of their neighbours' plus a small personal adjustment. Nobody can compute the final settings directly, but each evening every household looks at its neighbours' current settings and updates its own. Day by day the settings stop changing: they have converged to the solution of a linear system, without anyone ever writing the system down. This is the <strong>Jacobi method</strong>. If households update one at a time, each using the newest values of the neighbours who already updated, that is <strong>Gauss–Seidel</strong>, and it usually settles faster.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-jacobi-method\" data-h=\"3\">The Jacobi method</h3>\n<p>Write the equations so that each one solves for its own diagonal unknown. For the system\n<span class=\"mb\">4x + y = 9,\\qquad x + 3y = 5,</span>\nwhose solution is <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>, rewrite as <span class=\"m\">x = \\frac{9 - y}{4}</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac{5 - x}{3}</span>. Jacobi plugs the <em>old</em> values into the right sides. From <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>: <span class=\"m\">(x_1, y_1) = (2.25, 1.667)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">(1.833, 0.917)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">(2.021, 1.056)</span>. The iterates circle in on <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>, the error shrinking by a factor of about <span class=\"m\">0.29</span> each step.</p>\n<h3 id=\"gauss-seidel\" data-h=\"3\">Gauss–Seidel</h3>\n<p>Gauss–Seidel uses each new value as soon as it is available. From <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 2.25</span>, then immediately <span class=\"m\">y_1 = \\frac{5 - 2.25}{3} = 0.917</span>. Next <span class=\"m\">x_2 = \\frac{9 - 0.917}{4} = 2.021</span>, <span class=\"m\">y_2 = \\frac{5 - 2.021}{3} = 0.993</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">x_3 = 2.0017</span>, <span class=\"m\">y_3 = 0.9994</span>. For this system each step cuts the error by about <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{12} \\approx 0.083</span>, the square of Jacobi's factor. It also needs only one copy of the solution vector in memory.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Iterative method</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A method that produces a sequence of approximations <span class=\"m\">x^{(0)}, x^{(1)}, \\ldots</span> converging to the solution of <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span>, typically using only matrix-vector products. Examples: Jacobi, Gauss–Seidel, conjugate gradient.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"when-do-they-converge\" data-h=\"3\">When do they converge?</h3>\n<p>Both methods have the form <span class=\"m\">x^{(k+1)} = Gx^{(k)} + c</span> for an <strong>iteration matrix</strong> <span class=\"m\">G</span>. The error obeys <span class=\"m\">e^{(k+1)} = Ge^{(k)}</span>, so it shrinks to zero for every starting guess exactly when the <strong>spectral radius</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\rho(G)</span>, the largest absolute eigenvalue of <span class=\"m\">G</span>, is less than 1. Then the error falls roughly by a factor <span class=\"m\">\\rho(G)</span> per step. For Jacobi on our system, <span class=\"m\">G = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -\\frac14\\\\-\\frac13 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>, whose eigenvalues are <span class=\"m\">\\pm\\sqrt{1/12} \\approx \\pm0.289</span>. A simple sufficient condition is <strong>strict diagonal dominance</strong>: each diagonal entry is larger in absolute value than the sum of the others in its row (<span class=\"m\">4 &gt; 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">3 &gt; 1</span> here).</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Spectral radius</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\rho(G) = \\max|\\lambda_i(G)|</span>, the largest absolute eigenvalue. A stationary iteration <span class=\"m\">x \\leftarrow Gx + c</span> converges for every start if and only if <span class=\"m\">\\rho(G) &lt; 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"conjugate-gradients\" data-h=\"3\">Conjugate gradients</h3>\n<p>For symmetric positive definite systems the star method is <strong>conjugate gradients</strong> (CG). Solving <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> is the same as minimizing the quadratic <span class=\"m\">\\frac12x^TAx - b^Tx</span>, whose gradient is <span class=\"m\">Ax - b</span>. Plain gradient descent zig-zags down narrow valleys. CG instead chooses each new search direction to be \"conjugate\" to all the previous ones, so it never undoes earlier progress. In exact arithmetic it finds the exact solution in at most <span class=\"m\">n</span> steps, and in practice it gets a good answer far sooner. Its error shrinks by about <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sqrt\\kappa - 1}{\\sqrt\\kappa + 1}</span> per step, compared with <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\kappa - 1}{\\kappa + 1}</span> for gradient descent with the best fixed step. With <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = 100</span>, that is <span class=\"m\">0.82</span> per step for CG versus <span class=\"m\">0.98</span> for gradient descent. <strong>Preconditioning</strong>, multiplying by an easy approximation of <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span> to reduce <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span>, makes CG faster still.</p>\n<h3 id=\"power-iteration-for-eigenvectors\" data-h=\"3\">Power iteration for eigenvectors</h3>\n<p>The same idea of repeated multiplication finds eigenvectors. <strong>Power iteration</strong> starts from any vector and repeatedly multiplies by <span class=\"m\">A</span> and rescales. Each multiplication stretches the component along the dominant eigenvector more than the others, so the direction converges to it. Take <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, with eigenvalues 3 and 1 and dominant eigenvector <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>. From <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>: <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(5, 4)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(14, 13)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(41, 40)</span>. The entries quickly become nearly equal. The <strong>Rayleigh quotient</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\frac{x^TAx}{x^Tx}</span> estimates the eigenvalue: for <span class=\"m\">x = (14, 13)</span> it is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1094}{365} \\approx 2.997</span>. The error shrinks by the ratio <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1| = \\frac13</span> per step, so close eigenvalues mean slow convergence.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Power iteration</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The iteration <span class=\"m\">x_{k+1} = Ax_k/\\|Ax_k\\|</span>, which converges to the eigenvector of the largest-magnitude eigenvalue when that eigenvalue is strictly dominant, at rate <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1|</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Running Jacobi or Gauss–Seidel on a matrix with <span class=\"m\">\\rho(G) \\ge 1</span>. The iterates diverge; check diagonal dominance or use CG or GMRES.</li>\n<li>Using CG on a matrix that isn't symmetric positive definite.</li>\n<li>Stopping when consecutive iterates are close rather than checking the residual <span class=\"m\">\\|b - Ax\\|</span>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to normalize in power iteration, so the numbers overflow or underflow.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Iterative methods solve huge sparse systems with matrix-vector products. Jacobi updates every unknown from old values; Gauss–Seidel uses new values immediately and is often twice as fast. Stationary methods converge if and only if the spectral radius of the iteration matrix is below 1, which diagonal dominance guarantees. Conjugate gradients solve SPD systems at a rate set by <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt\\kappa</span>, and power iteration finds the dominant eigenvector at rate <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1|</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"eigen\" data-args='{&quot;a&quot;: 2, &quot;b&quot;: 1, &quot;c&quot;: 1, &quot;d&quot;: 2}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Rotate x until Ax lies along x; power iteration finds the dominant one automatically.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, drag x around the circle until the widget reports an eigenvector. You should find the direction <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, -1)</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 1</span>.\n2. Put x at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> and note where <span class=\"m\">Ax = (2, 1)</span> points. Now move x to that direction: <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> moves even closer to <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>. You are doing power iteration by hand.\n3. Change <span class=\"m\">d</span> so the two eigenvalues get closer together and repeat step 2; each step gains less.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> repeatedly applying <span class=\"m\">A</span> pulls every direction toward the dominant eigenvector, faster when <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1|</span> is small. That ratio is the convergence rate from the article.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;ravine&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.2}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Gradient descent on an ill-conditioned quadratic: the zig-zag that conjugate gradients avoid.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run with the default learning rate and watch the path bounce across the narrow valley while crawling along it.\n2. Lower the learning rate: the bouncing stops but progress along the valley becomes very slow.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the curvatures are 0.4 and 8, a condition number of 20. Gradient descent's speed is limited by <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span>, while conjugate gradients would reach the minimum of this two-dimensional quadratic in exactly 2 steps.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Jacobi: } x_i^{(k+1)} = \\frac{1}{a_{ii}}\\Big(b_i - \\sum_{j \\ne i}a_{ij}x_j^{(k)}\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Gauss–Seidel: } x_i^{(k+1)} = \\frac{1}{a_{ii}}\\Big(b_i - \\sum_{j &lt; i}a_{ij}x_j^{(k+1)} - \\sum_{j &gt; i}a_{ij}x_j^{(k)}\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">e^{(k+1)} = Ge^{(k)},\\quad \\text{converges} \\iff \\rho(G) &lt; 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CG rate} \\approx \\frac{\\sqrt\\kappa - 1}{\\sqrt\\kappa + 1},\\qquad \\text{GD rate} \\approx \\frac{\\kappa - 1}{\\kappa + 1}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x_{k+1} = \\frac{Ax_k}{\\|Ax_k\\|},\\qquad \\lambda \\approx \\frac{x^TAx}{x^Tx}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobi converges for strictly diagonally dominant matrices</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">|a_{ii}| &gt; \\sum_{j \\ne i}|a_{ij}|</span> for every row <span class=\"m\">i</span>, the Jacobi iteration converges to the solution of <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> from any starting vector.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Jacobi iteration matrix has entries <span class=\"m\">G_{ij} = -a_{ij}/a_{ii}</span> for <span class=\"m\">j \\ne i</span> and <span class=\"m\">G_{ii} = 0</span>. Its row-sum norm is <span class=\"m\">\\|G\\|_\\infty = \\max_i\\sum_{j \\ne i}\\frac{|a_{ij}|}{|a_{ii}|} &lt; 1</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">e^{(k+1)} = Ge^{(k)}</span>, we get <span class=\"m\">\\|e^{(k)}\\|_\\infty \\le \\|G\\|_\\infty^k\\|e^{(0)}\\|_\\infty \\to 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Convergence of power iteration</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A</span> has eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_1| &gt; |\\lambda_2| \\ge \\cdots</span> with a full set of eigenvectors <span class=\"m\">v_i</span>, and the start <span class=\"m\">x_0</span> has a nonzero component along <span class=\"m\">v_1</span>, then the direction of <span class=\"m\">A^kx_0</span> converges to <span class=\"m\">v_1</span>, with error proportional to <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1|^k</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Write <span class=\"m\">x_0 = c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \\cdots</span> with <span class=\"m\">c_1 \\ne 0</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">A^kx_0 = \\lambda_1^k\\big(c_1v_1 + c_2(\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1)^kv_2 + \\cdots\\big)</span>. Every ratio <span class=\"m\">(\\lambda_i/\\lambda_1)^k</span> for <span class=\"m\">i \\ge 2</span> tends to 0 at least as fast as <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1|^k</span>, so after rescaling only the <span class=\"m\">v_1</span> term survives.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Two Jacobi steps for 4x + y = 9, x + 3y = 5 from (0, 0)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Step 1: <span class=\"m\">x = 9/4 = 2.25</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 5/3 \\approx 1.667</span>.</li>\n<li>Step 2: <span class=\"m\">x = (9 - 1.667)/4 \\approx 1.833</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = (5 - 2.25)/3 \\approx 0.917</span>.</li>\n<li>The exact solution is <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>; the error went from <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> to about <span class=\"m\">(0.25, 0.67)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(0.17, 0.08)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Spectral radius of the Jacobi iteration matrix for the same system</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">G = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -1/4\\\\-1/3 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\det(G - \\lambda I) = \\lambda^2 - \\frac{1}{12} = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = \\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12}}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\rho(G) \\approx 0.289 &lt; 1</span>: Jacobi converges, gaining about half a digit per step. Gauss–Seidel's rate here is <span class=\"m\">\\rho^2 = \\frac{1}{12}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Apply power iteration to <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> from <span class=\"m\">x_0 = (1, 0)</span>, scaling so the first entry is 1 after each step. (a) Do three steps. (b) Estimate the dominant eigenvalue. (c) The eigenvalues are 5 and 2; how fast does the error shrink?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">Ax_0 = (4, 2) \\to x_1 = (1, 0.5)</span>. <span class=\"m\">Ax_1 = (4.5, 3.5) \\to x_2 = (1, 0.778)</span>. <span class=\"m\">Ax_2 = (4.778, 4.333) \\to x_3 = (1, 0.907)</span>. The direction approaches <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>. (b) The scale factors 4, 4.5, 4.78 approach 5; indeed <span class=\"m\">A(1, 1) = (5, 5)</span>. (c) The error shrinks by about <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1| = \\frac25 = 0.4</span> per step: check <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.5 = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.778 = 0.222</span>, <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.907 = 0.093</span>, ratios about 0.44 and 0.42, tending to 0.4.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>PageRank</strong> is power iteration on the web's link matrix: the dominant eigenvector gives each page's importance.</li>\n<li><strong>Spectral normalization</strong> in GANs estimates each weight matrix's largest singular value with one step of power iteration per training step.</li>\n<li><strong>PCA on huge datasets</strong> uses Lanczos or randomized methods, sophisticated relatives of power iteration, to find the top eigenvectors of the covariance matrix.</li>\n<li><strong>Hessian-free optimization and Gaussian processes</strong> solve large SPD systems with preconditioned conjugate gradients, using only Hessian-vector or kernel-vector products.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Simulations of heat flow, electric fields and fluids produce sparse systems with millions of unknowns, solved by preconditioned CG, GMRES or multigrid. Multigrid applies a few Gauss–Seidel sweeps on grids of several resolutions and can solve such systems in time proportional to the number of unknowns.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Search engines rank pages, and social networks rank influential accounts, with eigenvector computations performed by power-iteration-style algorithms on graphs with billions of nodes, where a direct method would be impossible.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">First Jacobi step for 4x + y = 9 from (x, y) = (0, 0): what is x?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2.25|9/4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">x = (9 - 0)/4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A stationary iteration converges for every start exactly when the spectral radius of G is:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Greater than 1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Less than 1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Equal to 1</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The error is multiplied by <span class=\"m\">G</span> each step.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Power iteration on a matrix with eigenvalues 3 and 1: by what factor does the error shrink per step?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/3|0.333\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_2/\\lambda_1| = \\frac13</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is the matrix [[4, 1], [1, 3]] strictly diagonally dominant?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4 &gt; 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">3 &gt; 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In exact arithmetic, conjugate gradients solves an n × n SPD system in at most how many steps?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">n</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">n³</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each step eliminates the error along a new conjugate direction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Rayleigh quotient xᵀAx / xᵀx for A = [[2, 1], [1, 2]] and x = (1, 1)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">Ax = (3, 3)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{6}{2} = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "monte-carlo": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"using-randomness-to-compute\" data-h=\"3\">Using randomness to compute</h3>\n<p>It sounds backwards to use randomness to get a precise answer, but it is one of the most powerful ideas in computing. <strong>Monte Carlo methods</strong> estimate a quantity by simulating many random samples and averaging. They were developed in the 1940s by Stanislaw Ulam and John von Neumann while working on nuclear physics, and named after the Monte Carlo casino. Today they price financial options, render the lighting in animated films, quantify risk, fit Bayesian models and train neural networks: every stochastic gradient is a Monte Carlo estimate. Their great strength is that their accuracy does not get worse as the number of dimensions grows, which is exactly where the grid methods of the quadrature lesson fail.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you want to know the area of an oddly shaped lake on a map. You put the map inside a 1 km by 1 km square and throw 1000 darts at random, uniformly over the square. If 300 darts land in the lake, a sensible estimate of its area is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{300}{1000}\\times1 = 0.3</span> km². You never needed a formula for the lake's outline, only the ability to check whether a point is inside it. Throw more darts and the estimate gets better. How much better, and how fast, is the main question of this lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"estimating-with-darts\" data-h=\"3\">Estimating π with darts</h3>\n<p>Let's make the darts concrete. Pick points <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> uniformly in the unit square <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]^2</span>. The quarter-disc <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 \\le 1</span> has area <span class=\"m\">\\frac\\pi4 \\approx 0.785</span>, so about 78.5% of the points land inside it. If <span class=\"m\">k</span> of <span class=\"m\">n</span> points land inside, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac kn \\approx \\frac\\pi4</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\pi \\approx \\frac{4k}{n}</span>. With <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span> and <span class=\"m\">k = 785</span> we would get <span class=\"m\">3.140</span>; a typical run gives something like <span class=\"m\">3.10</span> or <span class=\"m\">3.18</span>. It works, but slowly, as we'll see.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-general-recipe\" data-h=\"3\">The general recipe</h3>\n<p>Most quantities of interest can be written as an <strong>expected value</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\mu = E[f(X)]</span> of some function of a random variable <span class=\"m\">X</span>. An integral <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1 g(x)\\,dx</span> is <span class=\"m\">E[g(U)]</span> with <span class=\"m\">U</span> uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]</span>, and the darts estimate is <span class=\"m\">E[\\mathbf 1\\{\\text{inside}\\}]</span>. The <strong>Monte Carlo estimator</strong> draws independent samples <span class=\"m\">X_1, \\ldots, X_n</span> and averages:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat\\mu_n = \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(X_i).</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1 x^2\\,dx = \\frac13</span> with three random points <span class=\"m\">0.2</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.5</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.9</span>, the estimate is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.04 + 0.25 + 0.81}{3} \\approx 0.367</span>. With thousands of points it would be close to <span class=\"m\">0.333</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Monte Carlo estimator</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\hat\\mu_n = \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(X_i)</span> for independent samples <span class=\"m\">X_i</span>, an estimate of <span class=\"m\">\\mu = E[f(X)]</span>. It is unbiased, and the law of large numbers guarantees <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\mu_n \\to \\mu</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works-and-how-accurate-it-is\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works, and how accurate it is</h3>\n<p>Two theorems from probability do the work. The <strong>law of large numbers</strong> says the average of independent samples converges to the expected value, so the estimate is consistent. Its <strong>variance</strong> tells us how far off it typically is. Each sample has variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2 = \\text{Var}(f(X))</span>, and averaging <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent ones divides the variance by <span class=\"m\">n</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Var}(\\hat\\mu_n) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n},\\qquad \\text{standard error} = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.</span>\nThe <strong>central limit theorem</strong> adds that the error is approximately normal, so a 95% confidence interval is <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\mu_n \\pm 1.96\\frac{\\hat\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>, using the sample standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\sigma</span>. Monte Carlo doesn't just give an answer; it tells you how much to trust it.</p>\n<p>For the π darts, each sample is <span class=\"m\">4</span> or <span class=\"m\">0</span> with <span class=\"m\">P(4) = p = \\frac\\pi4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 4\\sqrt{p(1 - p)} \\approx 4(0.41) = 1.64</span>. With <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span> the standard error is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1.64}{\\sqrt{1000}} \\approx 0.052</span>, which is why runs land around <span class=\"m\">3.14 \\pm 0.1</span>. With <span class=\"m\">n = 100{,}000</span> it is about <span class=\"m\">0.005</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-square-root-law\" data-h=\"3\">The square-root law</h3>\n<p>The error falls like <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}</span>. That is slow: <strong>to halve the error you need four times as many samples, and to gain one more decimal digit you need a hundred times as many.</strong> Simpson's rule in one dimension gains four digits when you multiply the points by ten. So for smooth one-dimensional integrals, Monte Carlo is a poor choice. But the rate <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span> does not depend on the dimension at all. For an integral over 100 dimensions, a grid with only 2 points per axis needs <span class=\"m\">2^{100}</span> evaluations, while Monte Carlo with a million samples still gives an error of about <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma}{1000}</span>. In high dimensions Monte Carlo is often the only option.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Standard error</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The standard deviation of an estimator; for a Monte Carlo average of <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent samples it is <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt n</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"variance-reduction\" data-h=\"3\">Variance reduction</h3>\n<p>Since the error is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>, we can improve it either by increasing <span class=\"m\">n</span> or by decreasing <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span>. Reducing <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> is often far cheaper. <strong>Antithetic sampling</strong> pairs each sample <span class=\"m\">U</span> with <span class=\"m\">1 - U</span>, so that errors partly cancel. <strong>Control variates</strong> subtract a correlated quantity whose expectation is known exactly. <strong>Stratified sampling</strong> splits the domain into pieces and samples each one, so no region is accidentally missed. A tenfold reduction in <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> is worth a hundredfold increase in samples.</p>\n<h3 id=\"importance-sampling\" data-h=\"3\">Importance sampling</h3>\n<p>Some expectations are dominated by rare events. The probability that a standard normal exceeds 4 is about <span class=\"m\">3.2 \\times 10^{-5}</span>; with plain sampling you would see about 3 such events in 100,000 draws, giving a very noisy estimate. <strong>Importance sampling</strong> draws from a different distribution <span class=\"m\">q</span> that produces the important region more often, then corrects with weights:\n<span class=\"mb\">E_p[f(X)] = E_q\\left[f(X)\\frac{p(X)}{q(X)}\\right] \\approx \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(X_i)\\frac{p(X_i)}{q(X_i)},\\quad X_i \\sim q.</span>\nSampling from a normal centred at 4 makes about half the draws land beyond 4, and the weights <span class=\"m\">\\frac{p}{q}</span> shrink each contribution to the right size. A good <span class=\"m\">q</span> can reduce variance by many orders of magnitude; a bad one, with lighter tails than <span class=\"m\">p</span>, can make it infinite.</p>\n<h3 id=\"markov-chain-monte-carlo\" data-h=\"3\">Markov chain Monte Carlo</h3>\n<p>Sometimes we can't sample from the distribution directly, for example a Bayesian posterior known only up to a constant. <strong>Markov chain Monte Carlo</strong> (MCMC) builds a random walk whose long-run distribution is the target, such as the Metropolis algorithm, which proposes a move and accepts it with probability <span class=\"m\">\\min(1, \\frac{p(\\text{new})}{p(\\text{old})})</span>. Averages along the walk then estimate expectations, at the cost of samples that are correlated rather than independent.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Reporting a Monte Carlo estimate without its standard error.</li>\n<li>Expecting doubling the samples to halve the error. It reduces it by only <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt2</span>.</li>\n<li>Using a poor random number generator, or reusing the same seed by accident across experiments.</li>\n<li>Choosing an importance distribution with lighter tails than the target, which can give huge or infinite variance.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Monte Carlo methods write the target as an expectation and estimate it by averaging <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent random samples. The estimate is unbiased, converges by the law of large numbers, and has standard error <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt n</span> regardless of dimension, with confidence intervals from the central limit theorem. Variance reduction and importance sampling shrink <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span>; MCMC handles distributions that can't be sampled directly.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"lln\" data-args='{&quot;p&quot;: 0.785}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Each run is a running average of random 0/1 outcomes, like darts landing inside the quarter-circle.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the true probability set to 0.785 (the quarter-circle's share, <span class=\"m\">\\frac\\pi4</span>), press <strong>Flip 1000 × 5 runs</strong>. Each line is the running fraction of hits; multiply by 4 for an estimate of π.\n2. Look at how far the five runs spread after 10, 100 and 1000 flips. The spread shrinks, but slowly.\n3. Compare the spread at 100 and at 400 flips: it should be roughly halved, since <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{400/100} = 2</span>.\n4. Set the probability to 0.5 and then 0.05. The relative spread for 0.05 is much larger: rare events are hard to estimate, the motivation for importance sampling.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> every run converges (law of large numbers), but the funnel narrows like <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}</span>. Getting one more digit requires 100 times as many flips, exactly the square-root law from the article.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"clt\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Averages of random samples look normal, which is what justifies Monte Carlo confidence intervals.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose the exponential population, which is very skewed, and set the sample size to 1. The histogram is skewed.\n2. Raise the sample size to 30 and resample. The histogram of averages becomes a bell curve, and narrower.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the width of the bell shrinks like <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>, and its normal shape is why <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\mu \\pm 1.96\\,\\frac{\\hat\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span> is a valid 95% interval for a Monte Carlo estimate.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\hat\\mu_n = \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(X_i),\\qquad E[\\hat\\mu_n] = \\mu</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Var}(\\hat\\mu_n) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n},\\qquad \\text{SE} = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">95\\%\\ \\text{CI: } \\hat\\mu_n \\pm 1.96\\frac{\\hat\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_0^1 g(x)\\,dx \\approx \\frac1n\\sum g(U_i),\\qquad \\pi \\approx \\frac{4k}{n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E_p[f] = E_q\\left[f\\frac pq\\right]\\ \\text{(importance sampling)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Unbiasedness and variance of the Monte Carlo estimator</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">X_1, \\ldots, X_n</span> are independent with the same distribution as <span class=\"m\">X</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\text{Var}(f(X)) = \\sigma^2 &lt; \\infty</span>, then <span class=\"m\">E[\\hat\\mu_n] = E[f(X)]</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\text{Var}(\\hat\\mu_n) = \\sigma^2/n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By linearity of expectation, <span class=\"m\">E[\\hat\\mu_n] = \\frac1n\\sum E[f(X_i)] = \\frac1n\\cdot n\\mu = \\mu</span>. Variances of independent variables add, and <span class=\"m\">\\text{Var}(cY) = c^2\\text{Var}(Y)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\text{Var}(\\hat\\mu_n) = \\frac{1}{n^2}\\sum\\text{Var}(f(X_i)) = \\frac{1}{n^2}\\cdot n\\sigma^2 = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Unbiasedness of importance sampling</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">q(x) &gt; 0</span> wherever <span class=\"m\">f(x)p(x) \\ne 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">E_q\\left[f(X)\\frac{p(X)}{q(X)}\\right] = E_p[f(X)]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">E_q\\left[f\\frac pq\\right] = \\int f(x)\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}q(x)\\,dx = \\int f(x)p(x)\\,dx = E_p[f]</span>. The condition on <span class=\"m\">q</span> ensures no part of the integral is lost.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Standard error of the π estimate with n = 10,000 darts</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Each sample is <span class=\"m\">4\\cdot\\mathbf 1\\{\\text{inside}\\}</span> with <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac\\pi4 \\approx 0.785</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 4\\sqrt{p(1 - p)} \\approx 4\\sqrt{0.785 \\times 0.215} \\approx 4(0.411) \\approx 1.64</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\text{SE} = \\frac{1.64}{\\sqrt{10000}} = 0.0164</span>, so a 95% interval is about <span class=\"m\">\\pm0.032</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Variance of the estimate of ∫₀¹ x² dx with 10,000 uniform samples</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2 = \\text{Var}(U^2) = E[U^4] - (E[U^2])^2 = \\frac15 - \\frac19 = \\frac{4}{45} \\approx 0.0889</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\sigma \\approx 0.298</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\text{SE} = \\frac{0.298}{100} \\approx 0.003</span>: the estimate is typically within about <span class=\"m\">0.003</span> of <span class=\"m\">\\frac13</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many samples to cut the error by a factor of 10?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\text{SE} \\propto \\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}</span>, so dividing the SE by 10 requires <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt n</span> to grow by 10.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">n</span> must grow by <span class=\"m\">10^2 = 100</span>.</li>\n<li>Going from 1,000 to 100,000 samples gains one decimal digit.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>You estimate the probability that two dice sum to at least 10 by simulating 3600 rolls. (a) What is the exact probability? (b) What is the standard error of the estimate? (c) How many rolls would give a standard error of about 0.001?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Sums 10, 11, 12 occur in <span class=\"m\">3 + 2 + 1 = 6</span> of 36 outcomes, so <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac16</span>. (b) Each roll is a 0/1 indicator with variance <span class=\"m\">p(1 - p) = \\frac16\\cdot\\frac56 = \\frac{5}{36}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\text{SE} = \\sqrt{\\frac{5/36}{3600}} = \\frac{\\sqrt5}{360} \\approx 0.0062</span>. A 95% interval is about <span class=\"m\">\\pm0.012</span>. (c) Need <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{\\frac{5/36}{n}} = 0.001</span>, so <span class=\"m\">n = \\frac{5/36}{10^{-6}} \\approx 138{,}900</span> rolls.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Stochastic gradient descent:</strong> a minibatch gradient is a Monte Carlo estimate of the full-data gradient, unbiased with variance shrinking like <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\text{batch size}}</span>. That is why doubling the batch does not halve the noise.</li>\n<li><strong>VAEs and the reparameterization trick</strong> estimate the expected reconstruction loss with a few samples <span class=\"m\">z = \\mu + \\sigma\\epsilon</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Policy gradients</strong> (REINFORCE) in reinforcement learning are Monte Carlo estimates, and baselines are control variates.</li>\n<li><strong>MC dropout and deep ensembles</strong> estimate predictive uncertainty by averaging several stochastic forward passes; <strong>Bayesian inference</strong> uses MCMC (for example Hamiltonian Monte Carlo in Stan) to sample posteriors.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>Monte Carlo tree search</strong> chooses moves in games by playing many random continuations and averaging the outcomes; combined with neural networks it powered AlphaGo. <strong>Randomized algorithms</strong> such as randomized quicksort, hashing and the Miller–Rabin primality test use the same principle: randomness makes bad cases unlikely.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Banks price complex options and estimate risk by simulating thousands of possible futures for interest rates and stock prices and averaging the payoffs. Animated films render light with <strong>path tracing</strong>, which follows millions of random light rays per frame; the grainy noise in an early render is Monte Carlo error that fades like <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}</span> as more rays are added.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">1000 random darts in the unit square, 780 inside the quarter-circle. Estimate of π?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3.12|78/25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4 \\times 0.78 = 3.12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">To halve the Monte Carlo standard error you need how many times as many samples?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\text{SE} \\propto 1/\\sqrt n</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt n</span> must double.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Each sample has standard deviation 2 and n = 400. What is the standard error?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.1|1/10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2/\\sqrt{400} = 2/20</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Three uniform samples on [0, 1] give f-values 1, 2 and 3. What is the Monte Carlo estimate of ∫₀¹ f(x) dx?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The average of the function values: <span class=\"m\">6/3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How does the Monte Carlo error rate depend on the dimension of the integral?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">It doesn't: the rate is σ/√n in any dimension</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It grows exponentially with dimension</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It is σ/n in one dimension and σ/√n in two</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Only <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> may change with dimension; the <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt n</span> rate does not.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is importance sampling mainly used for?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Making estimates biased on purpose</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Reducing variance, especially for rare events</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Avoiding random numbers</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Sample where it matters and reweight by <span class=\"m\">p/q</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "floating-point", "t": "Floating-point numbers", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">x = (-1)^s \\times 1.f \\times 2^{e}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{stored exponent} = e + \\text{bias},\\quad \\text{bias} = 1023\\ (\\text{double}),\\ 127\\ (\\text{single})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\varepsilon_{\\text{double}} = 2^{-52} \\approx 2.22\\times10^{-16},\\quad \\varepsilon_{\\text{single}} = 2^{-23} \\approx 1.19\\times10^{-7}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{fl}(x) = x(1 + \\delta),\\quad |\\delta| \\le u = \\tfrac{\\varepsilon}{2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{spacing near } x \\approx \\varepsilon|x|</span></p>"}, {"l": "error", "t": "Error, cancellation and conditioning", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{absolute error} = |\\hat x - x|,\\qquad \\text{relative error} = \\frac{|\\hat x - x|}{|x|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\kappa(x) = \\left|\\frac{x f'(x)}{f(x)}\\right|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{relative output error} \\approx \\kappa \\times \\text{relative input error}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sqrt{x + 1} - \\sqrt{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + 1} + \\sqrt{x}},\\qquad 1 - \\cos x = 2\\sin^2\\tfrac{x}{2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x_{1} = \\frac{-b - \\text{sign}(b)\\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a},\\quad x_2 = \\frac{c}{a x_1}</span></p>"}, {"l": "root-finding", "t": "Root finding: bisection, Newton and secant", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Bisection: } |x_n - r| \\le \\frac{b - a}{2^{n+1}}\\ \\text{(midpoint after } n \\text{ halvings)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Newton: } x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Secant: } x_{n+1} = x_n - f(x_n)\\frac{x_n - x_{n-1}}{f(x_n) - f(x_{n-1})}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Order } p:\\ |e_{n+1}| \\approx C|e_n|^p;\\quad p = 1,\\ 1.618,\\ 2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Newton error: } e_{n+1} \\approx \\frac{f''(r)}{2f'(r)}e_n^2</span></p>"}, {"l": "interpolation", "t": "Interpolation and the Runge phenomenon", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">p(x) = y_0 + \\frac{y_1 - y_0}{x_1 - x_0}(x - x_0)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p(x) = \\sum_{i=0}^n y_i L_i(x),\\quad L_i(x) = \\prod_{j \\ne i}\\frac{x - x_j}{x_i - x_j}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p(x) = f[x_0] + f[x_0, x_1](x - x_0) + f[x_0, x_1, x_2](x - x_0)(x - x_1) + \\cdots</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(x) - p(x) = \\frac{f^{(n+1)}(\\xi)}{(n + 1)!}\\prod_{i=0}^n(x - x_i)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Chebyshev nodes: } x_i = \\cos\\frac{(2i + 1)\\pi}{2(n + 1)}</span></p>"}, {"l": "num-calculus", "t": "Numerical differentiation", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h} = f'(x) + \\frac h2f''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{f(x + h) - f(x - h)}{2h} = f'(x) + \\frac{h^2}{6}f'''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{f(x + h) - 2f(x) + f(x - h)}{h^2} = f''(x) + \\frac{h^2}{12}f''''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h_{\\text{opt}} \\approx \\sqrt{u}\\ \\text{(forward)},\\quad u^{1/3}\\ \\text{(central)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Richardson: } \\frac{4D(h/2) - D(h)}{3}</span></p>"}, {"l": "quadrature", "t": "Numerical integration (quadrature)", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">M_n = h\\sum_{i=1}^n f(a + (i - \\tfrac12)h),\\quad E_M = \\frac{(b - a)h^2}{24}f''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">T_n = h\\left[\\tfrac12f_0 + f_1 + \\cdots + f_{n-1} + \\tfrac12f_n\\right],\\quad E_T = -\\frac{(b - a)h^2}{12}f''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S_n = \\frac h3[f_0 + 4f_1 + 2f_2 + \\cdots + 4f_{n-1} + f_n],\\quad E_S = -\\frac{(b - a)h^4}{180}f''''(\\xi)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S = \\frac{2M + T}{3}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_{-1}^1 f\\,dx \\approx f\\big(-\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\big) + f\\big(\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\big)\\ \\ \\text{(exact to degree 3)}</span></p>"}, {"l": "ode-solvers", "t": "Solving differential equations step by step", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Euler: } y_{n+1} = y_n + hf(t_n, y_n)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Heun: } y_{n+1} = y_n + \\tfrac h2\\big[f(t_n, y_n) + f(t_n + h, y_n + hf(t_n, y_n))\\big]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{RK4: } y_{n+1} = y_n + \\tfrac h6(k_1 + 2k_2 + 2k_3 + k_4)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Backward Euler: } y_{n+1} = y_n + hf(t_{n+1}, y_{n+1})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">y' = -\\lambda y:\\ \\text{Euler stable iff } 0 &lt; h\\lambda \\le 2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Global error: } O(h)\\ \\text{Euler},\\ O(h^2)\\ \\text{Heun},\\ O(h^4)\\ \\text{RK4}</span></p>"}, {"l": "num-linalg", "t": "Solving linear systems: LU, pivoting and conditioning", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">PA = LU,\\quad Ly = Pb,\\quad Ux = y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{cost: } \\tfrac23n^3\\ \\text{(LU)},\\ \\tfrac13n^3\\ \\text{(Cholesky)},\\ n^2\\ \\text{per triangular solve}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = LL^T\\ \\text{for SPD } A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\kappa(A) = \\|A\\|\\|A^{-1}\\| = \\frac{\\sigma_{\\max}}{\\sigma_{\\min}},\\qquad \\frac{\\|\\Delta x\\|}{\\|x\\|} \\le \\kappa(A)\\frac{\\|\\Delta b\\|}{\\|b\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\kappa(A^TA) = \\kappa(A)^2,\\qquad A = QR \\Rightarrow Rx = Q^Tb</span></p>"}, {"l": "iterative-methods", "t": "Iterative methods and power iteration", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Jacobi: } x_i^{(k+1)} = \\frac{1}{a_{ii}}\\Big(b_i - \\sum_{j \\ne i}a_{ij}x_j^{(k)}\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Gauss–Seidel: } x_i^{(k+1)} = \\frac{1}{a_{ii}}\\Big(b_i - \\sum_{j &lt; i}a_{ij}x_j^{(k+1)} - \\sum_{j &gt; i}a_{ij}x_j^{(k)}\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">e^{(k+1)} = Ge^{(k)},\\quad \\text{converges} \\iff \\rho(G) &lt; 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CG rate} \\approx \\frac{\\sqrt\\kappa - 1}{\\sqrt\\kappa + 1},\\qquad \\text{GD rate} \\approx \\frac{\\kappa - 1}{\\kappa + 1}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x_{k+1} = \\frac{Ax_k}{\\|Ax_k\\|},\\qquad \\lambda \\approx \\frac{x^TAx}{x^Tx}</span></p>"}, {"l": "monte-carlo", "t": "Monte Carlo methods", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\hat\\mu_n = \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(X_i),\\qquad E[\\hat\\mu_n] = \\mu</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Var}(\\hat\\mu_n) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n},\\qquad \\text{SE} = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">95\\%\\ \\text{CI: } \\hat\\mu_n \\pm 1.96\\frac{\\hat\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_0^1 g(x)\\,dx \\approx \\frac1n\\sum g(U_i),\\qquad \\pi \\approx \\frac{4k}{n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E_p[f] = E_q\\left[f\\frac pq\\right]\\ \\text{(importance sampling)}</span></p>"}]});
